Jon Wright <jwright@halcyon.com> writes:
> I have the part number of the fan; it is 12-40095-02.  I called 
> the organization formerly known as DEC Direct.  According to
> them, the part may only be obtained by/through field service.

  Are you sure that's the right part number?

> It's just a stupid fan.  I took it out all by myself and I can
> replace it all by myself.  So I don't want to pay a jillion
> dollars for a service all.  I am willing to pay a premium to
> order the thing from DEC to save myself time and to be certain
> the replacement is compatible.  

  Field Service also sells parts - you don't have to have a service call
done. Just call your local FS office and tell them you need to buy a part.
This is often handled by the same group that deals with "carry-in Servi-
Center" stuff - the regular FS people may not be aware of it.

  However, my experience has been that when you order fans for older
equipment from DEC, you sometimes get used ones (this happened to me when
I ordered fan trays for DECserver 200's). Given that the parts warranty
is only 90 days and DEC marks up the price considerably, it's often bet-
ter to buy replacement fans on the general market.

  Most DEC fans are normal fans and you can get an exact replacement.
Just look for any markings that identify the vendor and model number.
If you can't find any, you can often find a close substitute by matching
the physical size, voltage, and current ratings.

  Of course, using a non-DEC fan probably voids the warranty, but if the
unit was *in* warranty you wouldn't have this problem in the first place
8-)

