That explains it.  My experience is with the box with the handle on
the back (though I think there is a varient that is non-SCSI).  These
boxes do work, at least for me.

FYI: I've also put RRD42 CD-ROM drives into RRD40 cabinets.  You have
to take off the front panel (and I think you could cut out the front
panel and put it back on), but for devices that are only 1/2 high
these boxes seem to work reasonably well.  Getting the cable to
fit in on the back was a bit tricky.

I will repeat what I said before, in a different way.  If you need
a box for production systems, spend the money and get a good modern
SCSI enclosure and power supply.  If you're a hobbyist, or you are
mounting something you'd like to re-use and it's not critical to
your business if it's out of service for a while, and you don't
need something that will be covered by a warranty, then re-using
some of these old boxes is reasonable.

