Date: 1998/10/29
Message-ID: <363894B3.5C7D76E5@star.enet.dec_nospam.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 406429860
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <362F7CE7.BC067777@eddie.mit.edu> <0cSx$hP5lA$F@malvm3.mala.bc.ca> <36349EC9.A7203173@eddie.mit.edu>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: OpenVMS Engineering
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

Try SYSGEN HELP

Under PARAMETERS look at TIMEPROMPT

When VMS boots and finds something bad about the time (VMS/UNIX/NT
use the BBW differently), it prompts for the time.  If you set this
to zero, it will use the last time the system was booted.  You had
better then add something to your "script" to set the real time.

This is all the result of the time being stored in the BBW differently
for the various O/S's (sigh, I agree it's pretty dumb that we can't
use a common format, or have the console convert formats when the
O/S is switched).



