From: wilson@dbit.com (John Wilson)
Subject: DOS file device access from Ersatz-11
Date: 1998/10/24
Message-ID: <36320fe0.0@news.wizvax.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 404654767
X-Trace: 24 Oct 1998 13:35:28 -0500, dbit.dbit.com
Organization: D Bit, Troy, NY
Newsgroups: vmsnet.pdp-11,alt.sys.pdp11,comp.sys.dec

FYI, the "Download" area on www.dbit.com now has a new RT-11 device driver
named DO: (DO.MAC, DO.SYS, DOX.SYS) which allows direct access to DOS files
from RT-11 programs running under the Ersatz-11 emulator.  This means that
instead of having to use PUTR (etc.) to insert/extract files in your RT-11
disk image(s) before/after use, you can reference them directly by using
"DO:" as the device name, as in "TYPE DO:FOO.TXT" and "COPY BAR.LST DO:".

It works using RT-11's "SPECL$" device bit which means it acts more or less
like a tape drive, so you can only have one file open on DO: at a time (so
for example, "COPY DO:FILE1.TXT DO:FILE2.TXT" is *not* allowed since both
files are on DO:).  Only files in the current DOS directory, with alphanumeric
names that fit into the RT-11 6.3 filename syntax may be accessed.  There
is currently no way to do a directory listing (DIR DO: returns an error),
but this may be added in the future, by faking an RT-11 format directory
generated from the DOS one (DIR.SAV appears to try to read an RT-11 directory
from block 6, even though it's a SPECL$ device -- this is probably a bug but
it's been true since V4.0 at least, and in this case it makes things easier
than having to do a complete ANSI tape emulation).

This driver will work with all versions of E11 from V2.0 on (and versions
of RT-11 from V4.0 on), including the free demo version on www.dbit.com
(commercial users will receive the driver in the V2.0L update, shipped
yesterday).  It *may* also work with competing products made by Strobe
Data Inc., but such use is not supported by D Bit.  It will not work on any
other system, and certainly not on real PDP-11s, since it depends on a DOS
pseudo-device to access the files rather than knowing how to manipulate FAT
file formats directly.  Maybe in a future version!

John Wilson
D Bit



