In article <36314D08.741FE841@EasyInternet.net>,
Paul DeMone  <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net> wrote:
>
> Mr McCalpin's posts, like virtually everyone else's in newsgroups, is a
> personal opinion.  However, the sig he appends to his posts appears to
> play up his affiliation with SGI and his role there.  Great.  But given
> that prominence, I felt fully justified in reminding him of the past sins
> of his employer related to the R10K in response to his criticism of 21264
> performance claims.  

Apparently, I have still not made myself clear....

The original claims for the MIPS R10000 were "correct", in the
sense of being theoretically attainable, but it took a long time to
get there.  SGI reached the SPECint95 targets rather quickly (and
exceeded them ~1.5 years later), but took a long time, and the
introduction of a new system, to reach the SPECfp95 targets.  From
a marketing perspective, the original claims were probably
presented inappropriately, but that was before my employment at
SGI.

My rather long-winded commentary was suggesting that the Samsung
projections for SPECint95 may not be in the same category.  

There is no "magic bullet" in system design or L2 cache speed to
produce significant improvements in SPECint95.  Compiler technology
may or may not be adequate to get there.  To be blunt (again), it
depends on how advanced (and appropriate) the compiler technology
is today --- if it is really good, then getting 20% more
performance is unlikely.  Conversely, getting 20% more performance
in the future implies that the technology today is rather weak (or
not properly targetted to the architecture).  

Given the lateness of the EV6 processor, and the fairly significant
time that has elapsed since DEC (pre-Compaq) had silicon, it would
surprise me if they released SPECint95 numbers that were not based
on aggressively targeted and tuned compilers.  Of course, there are
always non-technical factors to consider -- like being bought by
Compaq. So we will just have to wait and see....


On the other hand (as I suggested as well), the SPECfp95
projections may be the beginning of a story very similar to that of
the MIPS R10000.  Given a larger and faster L2 cache and a new and
aggressively designed memory subsystem, the SPECfp95 estimate is
not beyond the realm of possibility.




>Well, that would probably pass the litmus test of political
>correctness and thus avoid crushing Mr McCalpin's self-esteem
>and sense of self-worth.

For better or worse, crushing my self-esteem would be a
significant undertaking.   :-)






>However, it is exactly the wrong message I wanted to send.  The
>degree to which vapour has been marketed by DEC with regards to
>Alpha has been historically much less than at other companies.  

Do you really think so?  I see DEC as having vigorously promoted
the myth that MHz == performance, despite large quantities of data
to the contrary.  The general rule is that a 250 MHz R10000 or 
a 240 MHz PA-8200 or a 160 MHz Power2-SC is roughly equivalent 
(+/- 20% for most number-crunching applications) in performance
to a 612 MHz 21164 (EV56) in an AlphaServer 8400 chassis.

If I had to pick a company that has a history of modesty with
respect to performance claims, it would probably be HP (though
that has changed recently --- especially in the workstation
and graphics spaces).


>Just as I detailed the sad and twisted tale of the R10K (I even
>abstained from shooting fish in a barrel and recounting SGI/MIPS's
>hilarious "the fastest CPU on the planet" marketing campaign)

I don't recall this one -- perhaps it was before my time....

On the other hand, the claim is not ridiculous -- in the last 9
months, SGI systems based on the 250 MHz MIPS R10000 processor have
held a large number of "world record" performance titles, including
fastest SPECfp_rate95 for a shared-memory machine, fastest web
server, fastest 100 GB TPC-D benchmark, fastest file server, and a
few others.  

Of course, many of these titles have changed hands several times
during this period, but holding them even for a while seems to make
the marketing claim something more serious than "hilarious". Still
inappropriately strong, I will agree, but not ludicrous.
