Jerome Fine replies:

First, let me say that I do not find your post offensive - it is how you feel
and I do not doubt that many RT-11 fans feel the same way.  To a
very large extent, I used to feel the same way about DEC and RT-11
as well.  So it is very easy to understand and you are entitled to your
viewpoints in all the other areas as well.

As far as Mentec is concerned and V5.7 and bugs, we will have to wait
until V5.7 is finally released  AND  see what warranties they provide.
But, since Mentec had the agreement and started to sell RT-11, has
Mentec fixed any of the bugs in V5.6?  Now I have never expected
Mentec to be responsible for or fix any of the bugs in V5.6 until such time
as V5.7 is actually released - or if Mentec did, I have not heard where
the bugs fixes might be available from Mentec - either free or to be paid
for - are the bug fixes available from any other source?  I raise the point
of the bugs not to point out that Mentec is less than reliable, but only that
Mentec thus far has acted as most companies act - based on the possibility
of making a profit - which is perfectly OK by most people!  As you have
stated:   "Love me (RT-11), love my bugs."

With regard to your feeling that I am flogging my stuff for V5.4G and
trying to to find out whether it is worthwhile doing the stuff before I
actually have done the work, or so your post seems to infer, let me
please point out 2 things:
(a) The 7 utilities which I stated were done (DIR, PIP, LINK, LIBR,
     IND, BUP, MACRO) have been completed and were in field test
     for a year before they were delivered over 6 months ago where
     they are now operating in a Nuclear Generating Station.  Along
     with the monitor files and tape device drivers, I do not feel that
     a trivial portion of the software which needs Y2K compliance is
     being offered.  And the rest could be done if there is any interest.
     As for an SPD and release notes, I will be glad to provide anyone
     who actually wants to buy the patches with the necessary
     information.  I remember the last time I was inquiring from Mentec -
     about one of the TCP/IP commercial products I think it was - on
     behalf of a customer who later decided to go with GPIB hardware
     between a PDP-11 and a PC because at $ 6,500 an hour in
     operating costs, they needed a faster protocol than kermit - Mentec
     said that the only information they would provide was the actual
     product - after full payment was sent.  I hardly think that my actions
     are in any manner in that category.  However, at the end of the day,
     the only aspect that matters is whether or not I receive any truly
     serious inquiries.  I will provide those inquiries with a multi-tier
     price selection based on their actual preferences which I will not
     be able to determine in advance.
(b) Since at this time, Mentec is allowing V5.3 to be used free of charge
     as a hobby version of RT-11 and Megan Gentry has asked that some
     consideration be given in recognition of that aspect, I have stated that
     so long as Mentec recognizes the lack of  Y2K compliance in V5.6 is
     a known "bug" which needs to be corrected, I will not hinder the
     possibility that some hobby users are willing to immediately pay the
     upgrade price to V5.7 which is, as far as I know, $ US 1600 for the
     full H-kit.  Eventually, and probably within a year, I will make available
     to the hobby users, if I am told there is sufficient interest, Y2K patches
     for V5.3 of RT-11 at least at the same level as for V5.4G of RT-11.

Note that more than 6 years have elapsed since V5.6 was released.  I
have been told that a number of sites have already been made Y2K
compliant by fixing the V5.6 files from the sources.  As such, these
utilities could not be released in the form of patches since the other
99.9% of the code could not be separated from the patches.  The
solution I have produced does not even require the original SAV
file for any utility until the actual patch file (SIPP command file) is
used upon that SAV file to produce the modified SAV file which is
then Y2K compliant.  Experienced RT-11 users who understand
how to use SIPP and BINCOM will appreciate this aspect.

For those who wish, refer to my original post on Monday, 25-Oct-1998
at 7:25:23 EDT.

Sincerely yours,

Year 2000 Solutions for RT-11 Operating Systems and Applications
(Sources not always required)




