>    VMS systems don't normally ask for the time when booting unless:
> 
>    1.) There's a hardware problem with the on-board clock ( dead battery? )

Well, I don't know how to check the battery.  There doesn't seem to be anything
about it in the manuals, or maybe I just can't find it.  How would one check
this?  

There is one bit of evidence that this might not be the problem:  When I
boot the machine from the Unix partition, it comes up fine, and knows what
time it is, to within a second.  Of course, most of the machines are off
by N seconds, for various values of N, but N doesn't change, even if I boot
VMS and then reboot to Unix. So the clock seems to survive boots just fine,
from Unix's viewpoint, but VMS always asks.

>    2.) The system parameter SETTIME has been set to 1 ( 0 is the default )
>        You can check with the SYSGEN program using the command:
> 
>         SYSGEN> SHOW SETTIME

Tried that; the result was:

AVMS1_V71$RUN SYS$SYSTEM:SYSGEN
SYSGEN>  SHOW SETTIME
Parameter Name           Current    Default     Min.      Max.     Unit  Dynamic
--------------           -------    -------    -------   -------   ----  -------
SETTIME                         0          0         0          1 Boolean    
SYSGEN>  


So number 2 is apparently not the problem.  This was done immediately after
a boot, and VMS did ask me for the date and time.

BTW, another VMS clock problem:  It seems to be confused by the change from
DST to standard time.  I gave it the clock time (EST), and after it was up,
typed in a "show time" command.  It gave the time as an hour earlier.  Oops!

There was no indication of time zone in its output, and there seems to be 
no way to tell it the time zone.  Or is there?  Where is it documented? The
"help" command doesn't seem to know anything at all about time zones.



