Hi,

I usually spend my time in solaris/aix/linux world, but I just got a DEC Unix
problem handed to me, and I have tried everything that I can think of to take
care of it, and have failed.

Here is my dilemna:

I have a DEC machine that needs to have 3 printers set up on it.  On one
printer (a printonix printer) is hooked up through the Parallel port, the
other two (an Apple printer, and an HP4000) need to have network
connections.

The problem with the printonix is that it prints every other character.  So,
for instance, the lptest looks something like this:

acegik. . .
bdfhjl. . .
and so on.

(I sort of suspect that there is something wrong with the printer, only it
works just fine on a different machine.)

The other two printers are network printers, I can ping them, but that is
the only network response that I can get out of them.

Here is a copy of my /etc/printcap:

lp0|0|test:\
        :af=/usr/adm/lp0acct:\
        :br#0:\
        :ct=/dev/lp0:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lp0err:\
        :lp=/dev/lp0:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/lpf:\
        :sb:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd0:\
        :sh:
lp2|2|apple:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lp2err:\
        :lp=:\
        :rm=APPLE_LW24289c:\
        :rp=text:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd2:
lp1|1|hplaser:\
        :af=/usr/adm/lp1acct:\
        :ct=tcp:\
        :if=/usr/lbin/hplaser4psof:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lp1err:\
        :lp=@hplasertest\hplaser:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/hplaser4psof:\
        :pl#66:\
        :pw#80:\
        :rw:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd1:\
        :xf=/usr/lbin/xf:

lp1 used to look a lot like lp2, but then I got to trying different things
out, and now it looks like this.

Thanks.

