In article <1dhb2fq.8b2k2x1lbz2o0N@pec-5.au3.m.uunet.de> wolfgang@amadeus.m.eunet.de (Wolfgang Keller) writes:
>
>A best-effort scheduler is nice, but it isn't perfect. I do know what it
>means to sit in front of a Unix workstation when someone has left a
>Netscape 'zombie' (for example) on it that eats up 99.99 % of the CPU
>and makes it impossible to do _anything_. Or when someone runs his FEA
>analysis job on my workstation instead of his. 

   A modern PMT scheduler will allocate approximately 1/N CPU to each
CPU-intensive same-priority user process being run.  If their process 
was eating up 99.99% of the CPU and your processes were getting the 
remainder, then your processes were *not* CPU-intensive, did *not* 
require any more CPU than they were given, and were blocked on entirely 
different resources.

  It is possible that other users were using several resources, lowering
your system's performance in general.  I take it you'd rather not let
anyone else use your desktop system, and do away with resource sharing
altogether (ie, lobotomize your system and make your organization less
productive as a whole)?  If you have root, then nuke the other users'
accounts.  If you don't have root, then that system was never intended 
for your sole use in the first place and it's not your place to bitch
about it.

  BTW, keep NT out of this; Microsoft technology is synonymous with
bad technology.  Using their broken software as an example of PMT is
akin to using SPARC as an example of a RISC architecture.

  -- TTK




