> Billy Y..

Thanks Billy ... We appreciate the support.  And you're right it will be
out Mentec's door and Digital's door within a few short weeks now.

RT-11 V5.7 has been confirmed to be compliant with the British Standards
Institute (BSI) published standard BSI DISC PD2000-1

Other standards are not met because of the order of components in the
date dd-mmm-yyyy (ISO uses yyyy/mmm/dd for example) and because of
date inferencing (continuing to allow 2 digit years for 1972-1999) and
the use of colons (:) in CSI date specifeiers.

To meet these other standards would have resulted in unacceptable
increases in code size and complexity.  Still, meeting the BSI Spec is
no mean feat and we are proud to offer that level of compliance
which will meet the needs of RT-11 users until 2099, and we are certain
that meeting PD2000-1 will more than satisfy the RT-11 user community.

We also went to great pains to ensure that untoward consequences did not
occur with date inferencing and other unusual date behaviour.  Dates
like 1:Jan:1 could have some interesting effects!  Because lawyers are
looking over the corporate world in the getting year 2000 compliance, we
didn't need accidents like
DELETE/NOQUERY/SINCE:1:JAN:1 that currently could occur in RT-11 even
now.  At the same time, we didn't want to eliminate the defaulting that
is permissible in the date string.  We belive we have met both goals.

Stuart



