X-Deja-AN: 404009474
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Organization: Digital Equipment Corp (slowly merging with Compaq)
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

The setup is a number of AlphaServer 800's that are accessible
over the net via their serial ports, which are plugged into a
VNswitch so we can telnet to them and do things remotely.  One
thing I need to do is to reboot them.  (Actually, it's really
an expect script that's doing the job, but the machine doesn't
know that.) Each machine has Unix, VMS, and NT partitions, and
are running Unix most of the time, but at times we want to make
them switch to VMS or NT.

With VMS, reboot almost works, except when it reaches the point
where it wants to know the date and time.  It asks on its local
console, so the remote user doesn't see anything, and has no
clue that VMS wants to know the date and time.

I've verified (by telnetting to the port myself and typing the
date/time string) that input from the serial port isn't accepted.
VMS remains hung indefinitely until someone walks over to its 
keyboard and types the date and time there.

Is there any solution to this?  Can VMS somehow be told to get
the timestamp from wherever Unix gets it from?  Is there some
special boot flag that will tell VMS to talk to the serial port
rather than the local display and keyboard?

We have this problem after a power failure, too.  The Unix
systems come up with no problems.  The VMS systems are all
hung waiting for someone to tell them the date and time, and
are dead to the net until they get an answer.

It seems there oughta be a way to do this across the net ...

(I seem to remember a joke about this a few years back, about
a new release of VMS that ran on a wristwatch-size PC that had
a tiny display and keypad.  When you flipped the display lid up,
VMS booted up, and the first thing it did was ask you the time.
I also heard the same joke told about MVS. I suppose if it were
NT, it wouldn't ask you the time; it would just restart at the 
time that you last used it, plus or minus an hour. ;-)



