kepe@ci1000097-a.nash1.tn.home.com wrote:
> 
> I just purchased my first alpha, just for beginers, and maybe to
> chug-a-lug rc5. I am also new to SCSI. So, when my Multia arrived
> I began loading the necessary hardware, Ram, HD, and ext. cdrom.
> The only problem encountered was "where is the power supply for
> the HD". The source power only comes with connectors for the mobo.
> From the reading I have done, what appears to be the power supply
> for the HD comes from the "backplane". I am assuming that the
> 4-pin male plug at the rear of the backplane is the location as
> stated by the material read. Can someone confirm/rebuke this,
> and if confirmed, what and where can this cable be purchased.
> 

There are different types of SCSI "riser" cards in Multias. One has a
PCI slot and works with a 2.5 inch SCSI hard disk. This appears to be
what you have, since the power to the 2.5 inch SCSI disk comes through
the ribbon cable. The other type of SCSI riser card is designed for 3.5
inch internal disks. It has no PCI slot, but it does have the standard
3.5 inch disk power cable.

If you want to buy a 2.5 inch SCSI hard disk, I have a used 540 Mb
Toshiba which I can sell you. You can also get them new (in sizes up to
1.2 Gb) from Bason Computer (http://www.basoncomputer.com). If you want
to get a different SCSI riser card, you might try where you bought your
Multa. Otherwise, try Starship Computer (http://www.starshipcorp.com).
