In article <362F7CE7.BC067777@eddie.mit.edu>, 
   John Chambers <jc@eddie.mit.edu> writes:

> 
> I've verified (by telnetting to the port myself and typing the
> date/time string) that input from the serial port isn't accepted.
> VMS remains hung indefinitely until someone walks over to its 
> keyboard and types the date and time there.
> 

     If you remove the keyboard the system will use the serial
port for all console I/O. Is that an option?

> 
> We have this problem after a power failure, too.  The Unix
> systems come up with no problems.  The VMS systems are all
> hung waiting for someone to tell them the date and time, and
> are dead to the net until they get an answer.
> 

   VMS systems don't normally ask for the time when booting unless:

   1.) There's a hardware problem with the on-board clock ( dead battery? )

   2.) The system parameter SETTIME has been set to 1 ( 0 is the default )
       You can check with the SYSGEN program using the command:

	SYSGEN> SHOW SETTIME

