Jerome Fine replies:

If you run RT-11 on an old PDT, then the DATE on the PDT is not going
to affect anything else, as far as I know.

>   But them I got to thinking,  I've got a slug of VAXes here, in a cluster,
> all disks connected to HSC50s, 70s and 90s. I'm wondering if the HSCs, that
> I've heard run a variant of RT-11, are going to deal well with Y2k.
>
>   The DEC Y2K web pages say they are status "B" (OK for Y2K), but, don't
> go into a lot of detail....anyone given it any thought?

When you say a variant of RT-11, is this an RTEM variant.  I am very
confused by your reference.  In particular, could this be an emulation?
If you are running the standard DEC utilities from RT-11, then an
emulation on a VAX is likely involved at some level.  Again, the DATE
likely does not matter. Until I understand exactly what is meant
by "a variant of RT-11", is is impossible to really say.

Incidentally, which version of RT-11 are you running?  The command:
"SHOW  CONFIGURATION"
will tell you as well as the banner when RT-11 first boots.

If you are referring to the EXCHANGE application which runs
under VMS on a VAX and allows access to the files written
by RT-11, I don't know if DEC is going to make EXCHANGE
Year 2000 compatible.  Does anyone else who is reading this
post know about EXCHANGE under VMS on the VAX and
the Alpha?  The last time I used EXCHANGE on a VAX under
VMS, it worked very well as far as the DATE was concerned,
but there was a huge deficiency in that EXCHANGE would not
reference more than the first (zero) RT-11 partition on the media
which was 300 MBytes and held 9 partitions.  By being very inventive,
I was able to place 8 contiguous VMS files in the exact locations
of partitions 1 through 8, although I did waste partition zero.
But, since there was only 5 partitions of data on the media, wasting
one partition to gain access to the other 4 was a huge bonus.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
Year 2000 Solutions for Currently Running RT-11 Operating Systems and Application Programs
(Sources not always required)




