Mark E. Levy <levy@family-safe.net> writes:
> You'll need more than just LAT, though.  Those terminal servers expect to
> load their software from a host that supports DEC MOP (Maintenance
> Operations Protocol), which, again, provides similiar functionality to
> BOOTP.  Without the load software, which, by the way, is licensed, those
> terminal servers are slightly smarter than a box of rocks, but not by much.

  Just to repeat and clarify:

1) There are MOP servers available for other environments than the ones DEC
provides. It's not particularly hard to write one, unless you want to deal
with all of the corner cases where DEC got cute with the protocol (DS5x0's,
PATHWORKS, cluster boots).

2) Each DECserver purchased from DEC came with a license. As an operating
system, it moves with the CPU and is not VMS, so there's no license trans-
fer fee. Therefore buying the media from DEC does not require proof of li-
cense ownership, and DEC doesn't ask for it.

  Neither of these points detract from the fact that these servers are use-
less without a host that speaks LAT. Of course, the DS100/200/500 technical
manuals are available from DEC, and between those, the MOP spec from DEC,
and the datasheets for the various chips, you could write a new download
image. I wrote one for the DS200 (but it was a configuration save/restore
tool, not a general server image), and I considered putting a stripped-
down 2BSD Unix on the DS550 to make it a TCP/IP terminal server, but never
got around to it (the boatload of surplus new $200 DS700's from Ireland 
sort of made the economics of using DS550's unattractive 8-).

  If anyone wants to have a go at it:

  DS100 tech manual: EK-DSRVA-TM, $43
  DS200 tech manual: EK-DECS2-TM, $42
  DS500 tech manual: EK-DECS5-TM, $84 (but the PDP-11/53, DELQA-SF, and
				  CXY08/CXA16 manuals would have more info)

