From: cornelius@mayo.edu
Subject: Experiences with DataRam memory warranties?
Date: 1998/10/20
Message-ID: <1998Oct20.184442.1@lpopsb>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 403375284
Organization: LPS
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec

An interesting package just arrived: an overnight express package from
DataRAM.

What's interesting about it is that we sent in the memory to be replaced,
along with a copy of our invoice from DEC for the labor in resolving the
problem, in May of 1997.  Now, a year and a half later it arrives, and by
overnight express.

No mention of reimbursing us for DEC's fees, but there is a letter asking
us for the original board that we already sent and threatening to bill us
for the replacement memory if we don't send it.

Anyone else have experiences with dealing with DataRAM on their memory
warranties?  Have they reimbursed for your labor charges, and if so, did
they drag their feet in doing it or did they pay promptly?

I have never understood unlimited memory warranties, or even five year
memory warranties, and have not been surprised to see memory manufacturers
who offered them in the DEC market go out of business over the years. But
DataRAM is still alive and well as far as I know, and it doesn't seem to
me to be unreasonable to ask them to live up to any commitments they made
when they sold us their product.

--
George Cornelius                    cornelius@mayo.edu



