In article <70jkht$21k$1@news.mel.aone.net.au>, 
geoffrobx@stmarksx.ppx.catholicx.edux.aux says...
> 
> Lee K. Gleason wrote in message ...
> 
> >  I mean the firmware for the HSCs, that controls the thing..not really
> >something I'm running...CRONIC they called it. It's RT-11 based,
> >mostly...probably enough so to have the same date problems...and
> >the HSCs know the date, they apparently get it from the VAXes in a
> >cluster...
> 
As far as I know, HSC's only use the date for display and error messages.
It doesn't come off the net; I think you have to set it manually when you
first boot them.  They seem to remember the date okay across reboots, 
power fails, etc.  The date has a 4-digit year, and seems to have the
same base date as VMS;  I've seen several HSC's that apparently never had
the date set, and were happily running in November or December, 1858, or
in early 1859.  They had been up for months with the wrong date, and it
never affected anything visible to the system users.

However, they do have a local tape (TU58 Dectape-II) on the HSC50's,
or floppy (RX33?) on the others.  I think the floppies (and maybe the 
tapes) are in RT11 format, and there are facilities to list directories, 
copy files, and run programs (e.g. diagnostics and exercisers) off the 
floppies.  I don't know if the dates stored in the file structure on
the floppies are meaningful, or just for display purposes, or what
happens if the HSC date is outside the legal RT-11 date range.  Most
likely, you get junk dates.  It probably doesn't break anything 
important.

