>> This SPECint95 estimate is not very credible.  Given the value
>> of 30 on the 575 MHz AlphaServer 8400-6/575, I would say that
>> 41-42 is more likely.  SPECint95 scales linearly with clock
>> frequency within a given processor family once the L2 cache
>> is 1 MB or larger.
>
>  The 8400 is far from an ideal box upon which to judge the true
>  performance capability of the 21264.  

Absolutely true.

But for SPECint95, it does not make much difference, as I explain below.


>  The 8400 EV6 CPU board
>  does not use the latest generation of high speed synchronous SRAMs
>  for the L2 and the memory system is suboptimal. 

This is not likely to be a significant factor for SPECint95.

There are two claims here: Cache SRAM speed and memory system performance.

Surprisingly, there is public data that you can use to
evaluate both of these items for yourself.  

A. Concerning cache SRAM speed, here are the two most directly
   comparable items:

(1) Compare SPECint95 for the SGI Octane (250 MHz) (=13.6) and
    SGI Origin2000 250 MHz (=14.7).    The *combination* of 
    increasing the cache size from 1 MB to 4 MB *and* increasing
    the speed of the cache from 167 MHz to 250 MHz changed the
    SPECint95 by only 8%.

    From my own experiments on the Origin2000, changing the 
    secondary cache speed from 125 MHz to 250 MHz (with a fixed
    size 4 MB cache), only changes SPECint95 by about 5%.

(2) Compare SPECint95 for the 400 MHz Pentium II with 0.5 MB L2
    cache (w/ECC) (=15.3) with the 400 MHz Xeon with 0.5 MB L2
    cache (=16.3).  The increase in SPECint95 by going from a 200 MHz
    L2 cache to a 400 MHz L2 cache is only 6.5%.

The MIPS R10000, Intel Pentium II, and Alpha 21264 are all out-of-
order processors.  I claim that unless DEC screwed something up
badly (very unlikely), the 21264 should be able to tolerate the
latency of slow L2 cache SRAMS for the SPECint95 benchmark.  I will
continue to make that claim unless John Henning tells me I am
wrong. :-)  Hi John!


B. Concerning memory performance, there is also some data available
   to the public.  But first, here is some from my magic bin of facts:

. SGI Power Challenge 195 MHz R10K/1MB L2: SPECint95 = 8.72
. SGI Power Challenge 195 MHz R10K/2MB L2: SPECint95 = 8.94
. SGI Origin2000      195 MHz R10K/4MB L2: SPECint95 = 9.48 <-- NOT CURRENT!!!

The Origin2000 result is the one from the launch in October, 1996,
and uses (approximately) the same older generation compilers as
the R10K Power Challenge results (which were never published widely).

The main memory latency on the Origin2000 is about 1/3 the latency
of the Power Challenge, and the single-processor sustained (STREAM)
bandwidth of the Origin2000 is about 65% higher than the Power 
Challenge/10K.  Despite these huge differences in the memory system,
the *combination* of the larger L2 and the faster memory system only
made a difference of 6% in SPECint95.

There is some Intel data available.  If I have remembered the Intel
chipset data correctly, the 440LX chipset runs the bus at 66 MHz, 
while the 440BX chipset runs the bus at 100 MHz.  Comparing SPECint95
results at 300 MHz shows a change of only 2.6% (11.9 vs 11.6), 
despite the STREAM bandwidth change of 35%-40% (estimated from various
results in the STREAM tables), and despite the tiny (0.5 MB) caches.

From this I conclude that SPECint95 is also almost independent of
memory system behavior.


So I have convinced myself (and maybe some of you) that SPECint95
on systems with 21264 processors will not be significantly altered
by either SRAM or memory system changes.  I do expect compiler
improvements -- it took us a while to get the integer performance
of the R10K up to where we expected it to be.  This is tough to 
quantify for an outsider....



In light of these facts, the Samsung announcement of 55 SPECint95
at 800 MHz seems unlikely in the near term.  A straight linear scaling
by clock yields 42.2.   Allowing 5% speedup for faster SRAMs and
3% speedup for a lower-latency memory system only gets you to
about 45 for SPECint95 --- almost 20% lower than the press release
claimed.  

A 20% speedup due to compiler improvements is not out of the
question, but out-of-order processors are supposed to make it
*easier* to compile, not *harder*!  (Maybe this statement makes
it obvious that I am not a compiler guy?)




>  Hey John, people in glass houses...

This has nothing to do with throwing rocks.  It has to do
with sanity checking on marketing claims.  In my opinion,
the Samsung claims fail those tests....  

P.S. Hyperbole seems unnecessary for the 21264 anyway,
since it has the highest SPECint95 of any existing systems.  
It will be interesting to see how close HP comes to their
claim of 30 SPECint95 for the 440 MHz PA-8500 and how soon
it actually ships....
