> From this I conclude that SPECint95 is also almost independent of
> memory system behavior.

   Yes, I agree that 21264 SPECint95 won't be bumped up a large amount
   by better L2 and memory performance.  I was thinking more in terms
   of the SPECfp95 performance.  The SGI experience with the R10K that
   I detailed in my previous post clearly showed how the first boxes
   suffered a significant shortfall in SPECfp95 performance from sub-
   optimal system design while SPECint95 was within ~10% of predicted.
   DEC is likely simply repeating this experience with the 21264.

> 
> So I have convinced myself (and maybe some of you) that SPECint95
> on systems with 21264 processors will not be significantly altered
> by either SRAM or memory system changes.  I do expect compiler
> improvements -- it took us a while to get the integer performance
> of the R10K up to where we expected it to be.  This is tough to
> quantify for an outsider....
> 
> In light of these facts, the Samsung announcement of 55 SPECint95
> at 800 MHz seems unlikely in the near term.  A straight linear scaling
> by clock yields 42.2.   Allowing 5% speedup for faster SRAMs and
> 3% speedup for a lower-latency memory system only gets you to
> about 45 for SPECint95 --- almost 20% lower than the press release
> claimed.
> 
> A 20% speedup due to compiler improvements is not out of the
> question, but out-of-order processors are supposed to make it
> *easier* to compile, not *harder*!  (Maybe this statement makes
> it obvious that I am not a compiler guy?)

   This is not out of the question.  HP released a new PA compiler
   around the end of 1994 that increased SPECint92 for 7150 boxes
   by 14% IIRC (nearly a decade after the compiler/ISA were initially
   designed!)

   The DEC compiler experience prior to the 21264 was based on
   optimizing and scheduling code for rigid in-order CPUs with
   restrictive superscalar issue.  Some time and experience will be
   required to tweak the compiler for the very aggressively out-of-
   order 21264 to run at its full potential.

> 
> >  Hey John, people in glass houses...
> 
> This has nothing to do with throwing rocks.  It has to do
> with sanity checking on marketing claims.  In my opinion,
> the Samsung claims fail those tests....

