In article <36302CF6.1B1D954F@EasyInternet.net>,
Paul DeMone  <PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net> wrote:
>John McCalpin wrote:
>> From this I conclude that SPECint95 is also almost independent of
>> memory system behavior.
>
>   Yes, I agree that 21264 SPECint95 won't be bumped up a large amount
>   by better L2 and memory performance.  I was thinking more in terms
>   of the SPECfp95 performance.  The SGI experience with the R10K that
>   I detailed in my previous post clearly showed how the first boxes
>   suffered a significant shortfall in SPECfp95 performance from sub-
>   optimal system design while SPECint95 was within ~10% of predicted.
>   DEC is likely simply repeating this experience with the 21264.

Of course.  If you re-read what I wrote about SPECfp95, you will
find that it is quite different than what I wrote about SPECint95.

I wrote:
>The SPECfp95 estimate is not out of the realm of possibility.  It
>will, however, require a completely new system -- this level of
>performance (80 SPECfp95) is not possible in a TurboLaser
>(AlphaServer 8x00) system for any foreseeable cpu speed.  Assuming
>Compaq sticks with a 4 MB L2 cache, simply upgrading the cpu speed
>from  575 MHz to 800 MHz in the AlphaServer 8x00 will only increase
>the SPECfp95 from its current value of 47.7 to about 58 (my estimate).

With a 4 MB cache, there is still a certain amount of data that
must be moved from memory to cpu and back.  No matter how much
you improve the cpu speed, the time to completion cannot get lower
than the time required to move that data.  In the AlphaServer 8x00
system, my estimates suggest that this will prevent Compaq from
attaining a SPECfp95 of 80 with currently foreseeable 21264 clock
speeds.

This may end up being quite analogous to the SGI experience with
the R10000.  The original implementation in the Power Challenge
suffered from both excessive memory latency and a severe limitation
on the number of outstanding cache misses (one!).  SPECfp95
increased from ~12 to 19 by putting the same processor into the
Origin2000, which was much better suited to the R10000: 2/3 lower
latency, 65% better sustained BW, and support for 4 outstanding
cache misses.  Some compiler improvements likely helped as well,
but I don't have any detailed info handly on that topic.



>> A 20% speedup due to compiler improvements is not out of the
>> question, but out-of-order processors are supposed to make it
>> *easier* to compile, not *harder*!  (Maybe this statement makes
>> it obvious that I am not a compiler guy?)
>
>   This is not out of the question.  HP released a new PA compiler
>   around the end of 1994 that increased SPECint92 for 7150 boxes
>   by 14% IIRC (nearly a decade after the compiler/ISA were initially
>   designed!)

And SGI increased its SPECint95 on the Origin2000 by 20% by
compiler technology improvements.  We will have to wait and see if
Compaq's compiler technology is also currently weak enough on the
SPECint95 front to allow such a large boost.  (Although my wording
may sound rude, this is not intended as a criticism of Compaq/DEC --
SGI, for example, targeted its early compilers pretty strongly at
floating-point applications.  This left lots of room for
improvement on integer apps, which we addressed with a new compiler
release in 1997 that gave the 20% boost I mentioned.)

