Jerome Fine wrote:

> I have tried to discuss only current issues with regard to
> how Mentec does or will support V5.7 of RT-11 and
> stay away from decisions that took place many years
> ago!  I did refer to what DEC used to do only to point
> out an example of what I hope Mentec will not do when
> Mentec is asked to fix the bugs in V5.7, as surely there
> will be, even if they are leftovers from DEC.  I feel that
> once Mentec actually releases V5.7, there is an obligation
> to assume full responsibility for all RT-11 bugs.  Does
> anyone else feel the same way?  Billy, do you feel that
> way - that Mentec should immediately fix all bugs left
> in V5.7 free of charge?

     I, for one, do NOT feel that Mentec (or any other software
company) has an obligation to "immediately fix all bugs left in
[the software] free of charge".  Now, if a vendor were to say
"This Software is Perfect, It Has No Bugs At All, and I Guarantee
That", then if you found a bug, you'd have a case.

     I'm reasonably sure that Mentec (and DEC, before them)
has made a reasonable effort to test RT-11 Version 5.7 as
thoroughly as they could, on as many different machines as they
could, at different sites, etc.  They have also tried to fix a known
collection of "bugs".  There are still (and will probably always be)
"missing features" (which, to some, might be a "bug"), and perhaps
hidden incompatibilities/inconsistencies in the internal design that
may cause a problem (one going the rounds involves specifying
the rad50 for "APR" as the year in a date command, something that
won't happen for quite a few decades, but is arguably a "bug"),
but at some level, someone (usually the vendor) needs to make a
sensible decision that "enough is enough, the product is stable, there
are no known serious problems, and we need to get this out the
door and to our customers".

     In my opinion, DEC has usually followed this policy with
RT-11 (and I have every confidence that Mentec will, also).  Yes, there
are/were things that didn't happen, "features" that were not fully
implemented (Y2K being an example), but most releases were, in fact,
remarkably stable, with little need for serious update bug fixes.

     As for myself, I'm anxiously awaiting the release of Version 5.7.
I do
hope to continue keeping my PDP-11s running well into the next
millenium.
If the Development Team and Testing Team are doing their stuff (and I
have
no reason to doubt it), I expect to be quite pleased with the result.

Bob Schor
RT-11/TSX+ User/Abuser




