Hi Jay,

Sounds like you have the internal "Backplane (internal SCSI bus)" DEC
Part # 54-22563-01 like I do.  Yup, the power feeds off the 4-pin on
the backplane.   You should be able to use a standard
"big-4-pin-power-connector  TO little-4-pin-power-connector" (you
know, like you use to attach a PC's power supply connector to the
little 4-pin connector on some 3.5" floppy drives) to connect a HD to
the Multia's pins (you may need to file-off the nub on the underside
of the connector cable you use to avoid bending the plastic on the
Multia's connector).

OR:

You might be able to get a SCSI cable from an electronics
store/computer store/"somewhere on the net" that has a male 50-pin
SCSI end and female external SCSI (2?) end.  This should allow you to
create an external connection from the soldered-in 50-pin SCSI
connector (female) to the outside world and any supported external
SCSI device (CD or pretty-much any SCSI hard drive you place in an
external case -- cases are available at reasonable prices).

OR:

I contacted Starship computer (www.starshipcorp.com) and you can get a
"Backplane (PCI slot, internal/external SCSI bus)"  DEC Part #:
54-22563-02 for the VX40 to replace your current backplane that will
give you a usable PCI slot (which will take up the space that would
have been used by an internal hard drive), and give you an external
SCSI connection for use with any supported external SCSI device.  Cost
as of today was: $49.95 US.


(I also have a couple of VX40's I bought from ONSALE -- will be buying
the part from starship for my babies.)

Happy UDBing!
Sandy


