peter@baileynm.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
> You could use union mounts. Union mount a new temp over the old one before
> you run the cleaner on the old one. Now the problem reduces to what happens
> when you run out of resources for the nth union mount. You'd need to be able
> to unmount stuff from the bottom of the mount stack.

Plan 9:

term% mkdir A B C D
term% bind -b B A
term% touch A/1
term% bind -b C A
term% touch A/2
term% bind -b D A
term% touch A/3
term% ls A B C D
A/1
A/2
A/3
B/1
C/2
D/3

However, union mounts apply to directories, not trees, so you can
still alter the contents of old subdirectories of B or C from A, which
isn't what you want.  BSD union mounts might work differently, more
like Sun's old translucent file system?

> If you have variable symlinks you can pull a nasty trick:
> 
> 	mkdir /usrtmp
> 	for user in ($USERS)
> 	do mkdir /usrtmp/$i; chown $i /usrtmp/$i
> 	done
> 	ln -s '/usrtmp/$(LOGNAME)/tmp' /tmp	# modulo variable symlink syntax
> 
> Now anyone accessing /tmp will find themselves in a private temp dir they own.

Plan 9 again; this is the actual convention:

term% grep tmp $home/lib/profile
bind -c $home/tmp /tmp

> Best would be a special tmpfs with file aging.

Plan 9 lets users mount anything they want on /tmp.  An instance of a
ramdisk, for example.  This and the previous example rely on a nice
property that Unix doesn't have: per-process namespaces.



