On Fri, 23 Oct 1998 20:00:47 -0500, turtle <china@radiks.net> wrote:

>	I have an opportunity to get one of these beasts for free and am
>wondering if anyone here can give me some information on the DEC Vax 6220

I have a 6220 here.  And a 6310, only difference no/type of cpu cards.

In brief.

It's a VAX.  It runs VMS or Ultrix.  Period. 
BSD or LINUX  is not supported on this model (yet). 
It has 2 cpus (in theory), more may have been added, or some
removed,  you need to look in the machine. 
It could have just one, or as many as 6.
It  would likely have 64mb of ram or better, again, you need to look.
It may or may not have an internal disk/tape controller card (KDB-50)
for RA series drives and TA tapes and/or a KLESI-B if it has a TU81+
tape drive. 
It may also have been part of a cluster on a CI Bus and it's drives
may have been mounted on a Heirarchical Storage Controller - a HSC50
or HSC70 most likely. A file cabinet sized box that uses about the
same amount of power as the VAX. (Usually a bit more in fact, YMMV)

You don't say if you are getting the whole system or just the machine.
The machine on it's own is not a lot of use, unless it has some drives
and a means of accessing them.
IF you are to receive an entire cluster and it's various bits, you
will need a light truck, a pickup say, ideally a forklift, (although
six medium sized blokes CAN handle it without too much trouble.) and
plenty of room. 
 If it has RA81/82 drives they weigh in at about 65kg+ each.  If
you're lucky you might get some RA90's which are smaller and lighter
(about 1/2 the weight) or best of all, some house brick sized RA7x
drives.  They will no doubt come in 5 foot 6 high cabinets and can
be best moved by removing the fairly fragile (as well as heavy!)
drives first.  
Make sure you get all the blue CI Bus cables and the Star Coupler if
it is a HSC system, and the black rectangular section SDI cables. 
If you can, try and get the manuals for the hardware and the O/S if 
they'll let you.  I had to put mine together and do mods without the
hardware books initially. 
If you have some VT100,200 or 300 class terminals that should get you
up and running.  You would need at least one for the console to be
able to boot the machine once you get it wired up.


>approximately 5' x 3' x 3' and is incredibly heavy.

The tape in the machine is a TK70 which is a 280mb (approx) cartridge.
It is used to load and O/S or boot diagnostics etc.
Yes. it's very heavy, about 300kg I think.

>	Basically what I am hoping for is some experienced insight on
>whether or not this unit is worth the hassle of moving even though it is
>free. Is it worth it to try to setup as a hobby machine.

Very likely, yes.  Despite the size, they are not mega consumers of
power, and I have modded these 2 for 240vac single phase.
You may have a little more difficulty unless you have 3 phase
available, as I am not sure how to do a mod for 110vac.  I believe it
is possible however.  Typically, these machines use about 4-500w.
(Mine draws 1.9a @240vac)

A typical(?) hobbyist setup might include the VAX, if it has a KDB50,
a drive or two and possibly a tape drive, with a couple of terminals.
IF it's a HSC system, you would need the HSC, a Star coupler, the Vax,
a couple of drives and a terminal or two (one for the HSC as well is
an idea)  
In either case, a couple of bookcases in size.

>possibly a chance of turning around and selling it to some group that
>would want it, etc. 

You wouldn't get much.  They go for about $400 a tonne around these
parts - scrap price.

>I have until Monday, Oct. 26 to make my decision.

If you aren't sure, and have the space, grab it, store it and think
about it.
Very likely if you don't want to tackle it, someone out there would
LOVE to get hold of it.  SIze notwithstanding.
Otherwise it's likely to wind up as dental fillings and razor blades.
Shame really.

Feel free to email me if you want more info/detailed answers.

Cheers

