In article <3629E05D.4A519AB9@trailing-edge.com>,
Tim Shoppa  <shoppa@trailing-edge.com> wrote:
>Is this the sort of situation that might call for a new CSI
>interface/specification?  Or is there a possibility for
>some unused bits to mark a RAD50 translation by .CSIGEN that
>would be backward-compatible?

Not to sound like Microsoft FORTRAN (which used a similar horrible hack
to retrofit CHARACTER*(*) subroutine parameters into their F77 *subset*
compiler at V4.0), but I guess it would be possible to define a static
array some place (maybe as a new RMON offset) that has a bit for each
switch in the most recent CSI call, saying whether it was numeric or
text (maybe two bits so you can tell octal from decimal too?).  That
would get messy if you had >1 job using CSI at once, maybe you could have
a .SCCA style call to specify where the bitmap should go.

>Does RSTS/E deal with this sort of situation differently, or
>does it simply follow RT-11's lead?

I'm many versions out of date with RSTS (saving up though!), back in
V7.0-07 days there was no DCL layer at all and RT-11's CSI caught the
command line (passed by RSTS CCL processing) verbatim.  When they added
DCL, they also added replacements for most utilities so I doubt there's
much translation going on (the utilities accept DCL style syntax directly),
except maybe for PIP.SAV which is too useful as-is to replace!

RSTS uses a hack similar to the above to store PPN information on .CSISPC
calls in some other buffer, so that if you pass a pointer into the .CSISPC
buffer for .LOOKUP/.ENTER it remembers the PPN too (so you can use directories
other than the current one even on non-RSTS-aware RT-11 programs), but its
RT-11 emulator is SJ only so you don't have to worry about multiple jobs
using the same buffer.

John Wilson
D Bit



