>any other the arrays are invalid the Alpha throws an exception while Merced
>only throws if the array should of be accessed. This was the point of the
>Merced example. Of course the example is not a good one since the arrays are
>almost certainly local and the compiler can determine that exceptions won't
>happen.

During the talk, Pete Bannon said that this code was generated when
the Digital Unix C compiler was supplied the -speculate flag.  This
flag causes the compiler to mark the generated code in such a fashion
that the OS throws away any memory access exceptions in the
"speculated" region of the code.  There are some correctness
conditions that the code must satisfy before it is safe to
supply the -speculate option when compiling.

Actually, I just attended a practice talk.  Presumably the
final talk also mentioned the -speculate flag.

