P Dickerson (peter@localhost) wrote:

: Slide 4 compares the 7 cycles of Merced code with 6 cycles of Alpha. However
: the Alpha code does not appear to have the correct exception semantics. If
: any other the arrays are invalid the Alpha throws an exception while Merced
: only throws if the array should of be accessed. This was the point of the
: Merced example. Of course the example is not a good one since the arrays are
: almost certainly local and the compiler can determine that exceptions won't
: happen.

Well, the fragment of code given on slide 4 apparently comes from the
8-queens problem (or at least the original Intel's example had). Using
8-queens as a benchmark had always sounded a bit silly to me: a properly
coded program solving it does NOT need any like extraordinary number of
memory accesses. In fact, the inner loop in the bit-array implementation
is simply a register shift-and-test (or find-first-bit if your compiler
is smart enough to figure that out), and can run as fast as the isolated
CPU core will let it.

With a pretty crude C implementation on a 500MHz 21164, it takes:

 Nqueens   Nsolutions   Seconds

    8           92        <0.01
    9          352        <0.01
   10          724        <0.01
   11         2680        <0.03
   12        14200         0.16
   13        73712         0.88
   14       365596         5.26
   15      2279184        33.51
   16     14772512       230.11
   17     95815104      1641.74

 ... and so on. (It is quite possible to go much faster with a bit of
a hand-tuned assembly code). I do not think you can beat these timings
using the naive implementation of slide 4 on however fast that 21364
(or first IA-64 chip for that matter) is going to be. So I guess the
only *real* point it makes is that an incompetent programmer can write
a molasses-slow program on any hardware regardless of how fast it is.

Cheers,

/Serge.P



