Stuart Brook wrote:
> 
> You haven't disagreed really with the point I was making ...
>

No, but there is still something nagging at me here - so i'll have
another shot...

Throwing a bit more into the pot - Languages exist to communicate ideas
and concepts right ? - this applies to programming language as well as
natural language. The more rich the language is in terms of its grammar
and vocabulary, the better it is able to do this. Languages so limited
tend to wither because it is more difficult to express ideas than with a
language having a richer grammar and vocabulary. The more more fluent
you become in a language, the better you become at expressing ideas
clearly and concisely. 

This brings me round again to attitude and education - Most professional
disciplines involve years of training and experience. Training that is
not just about the assimilation of facts, but the taking on board of a
set of attitudes and mind set. I know it's unfashionable, but part of
that mind set is about (dare I say it) pursuit of excellence, attention
to detail, making a system design elegant because it's cerebrally
satisfying, taking into account future maintenance requirements and all
the other stuff which has little or nothing to do with the
implementation language, but everything to do with the construction of
reliable and maintainable systems.

Now it seems like any fool who can write a 2 page utility in V Basic or
whatever instantly becomes a fluent programmer. Just because a hardware
engineer has been on a weeks C or C++ (even worse) course doesn't make
him a software engineer, though management sometimes seem to think so.
The result of this is that he is typically thrown in the deep end on a
project having learned the language, but having no training in terms of
program structure or global overview style system design. Small wonder
then, that we get disorganised, unstructured, bug ridden code that costs
a fortune to maintain. The guy is probably relieved just to get the
system working at all. This isn't fantasy either, i've seen the scenario
many times and not only in small companies. Both management and
engineers can be at fault - the management see a quick and dirty demo
working in almost no time at all and think "theres nothing to this, here
we have a product we can sell now", and engineers sometimes don't hold
the line for quality hard enough on the basis that it's less hassle to
promise the project manager timescale that they know can't be met
without compromising quality.

> C is NOT the panacea tool that it is cracked up to be

Agreed, but it's the closest we have to a truly general purpose
programming language, with enough high level constructs to allow order
to be imposed on a system design - *if* you take the time and trouble.
IMO, for embedded and systems programming work, it's close to ideal...

Rant mode off: *That* feels better...

Rgrds, Chris



