TO:  All Current User of RT-11 and TSX-PLUS

This post is divided into 5 sections.

1.  The Actual Announcement
     Patches are now available to be released to all commercial users of RT-11 to enable Year 2000
     operation of most (and probably all) of the most frequently used system and utility files for V5.4G
     and other versions depending upon the demand.  At present, the monitor files (including RT11FB
     and RT11XM) are Year 2000 compliant.  All of the tape handlers are also available.  And finally, seven of
     the most important utilities are available including:  DIR,  PIP,  LINK,  LIBR,  IND,  BUP and MACRO
     now have patches available so that they are Year 2000 compliant.  Note that depending upon demand, the
     other files such as QUEMAN,  SP(X).SYS and RTMON.REL  will also be made available.

2.  The Disclaimer
     (a) These patches have not been authorized by DEC/Compaq/Mentec.
     (b) The Year 2000 patches were not produced by DEC/Compaq/Mentec.
     (c) V5.4G of RT-11 is no longer supported by DEC/Compaq/Mentec.
     (d) The Year 2000 patches are not supported by DEC/Compaq/Mentec.
     (e) Obviously, any system which uses the Year 2000 patches is not supported by DEC/Compaq/Mentec.
     (f)  DEC/Compaq have documented the lack of Year 2000 compatibility for more than 20 years, but
           choose to ignore until around 1989 (with V5.5 SYSLIB subroutines) that RT-11 would survive after 2000.
     (g) DEC/Compaq/Mentec may choose to regard the overall lack of Year 2000 compatibility in V5.6 as
          something other than a known "bug" (maybe a feature) even though:
          (i)    Most (if not all) of the subroutines in SYSLIB.OBJ in V5.6 are Year 2000 compatible.
          (ii)   BUP  and  MACRO  in V5.6 are Year 2000 compatible (but they use only 2 digit years).
          (iii)  Mentec has publicly announced that a V5.7 with Y2K would be available for more that a year,
                 and with much emphasis on Y2K, but has not yet released V5.7 of RT-11 as of this post.

3.  The Historical Background
     About 10 years ago, I inquired about Year 2000 compliance for RT-11 directly from DEC.  At the time,
     I was making changes to an application which was running under RT-11. Since there were no plans at the
     time to complete the changes to make RT-11 Year 2000 compliant (they must have already been started
     since I understand that V5.5, which was released shortly thereafter, had some of the changes made to that
     version), I dropped plans to make the application Year 2000 compliant.  The application was scheduled
     to run until at least 2008.

     About 2 years ago, I started my own set of changes to make RT-11 Year 2000 compliant and most were
     complete about 1 year ago.  At that time, one of my customers decided that it would be inappropriate to
     depend on Mentec to release the changes in a timely fashion since the first release date of the summer of
     1997 had already been missed.  In April of 1998, I completed all the requested changes for the utilities
     which the customer required.  A full QA was done on the modified files and they were installed on a system
     about 6 months ago which is running in a Nuclear Generating Station (NGS).  Needless to say, I was
     particularly careful to ensure that I checked out the code changes since the NGS is less than 50 miles to
     the East of my home.  Indeed, the only complaint that I have had so far is that the DATE command
     allows values such as "30-Feb-2005", a feature which I had overlooked due to over 20 years of using RT-11.
     Since the system uses an internal clock card to set the DATE and TIME when the system is first booted, the
     setting of an invalid DATE value by a human operator was unlikely to occur and would not cause any explicit
     problems in any case.  Consequently, the Year 2000 patches have now been in use for 6 months.

     One additional factor which persuaded this customer is that no other code was modified except those portions
     directly affected by the need to make them Year 2000 compliant.  The RMON required only about a dozen
     instructions to be able to handle DATE values for after 31-Dec-2003, mostly in the DATE rollover portion of
     the code (which some users will remember is optional) in addition to a few other areas, two of which were
     noted by Megan Gentry many years ago which need to be present when DATE values for after 31-Dec-2035
     are being set.  Consequently, there is a very high degree of confidence that there are no additional bugs in the
     crucial operating system RMON code for V5.4G of RT-11 in an NGS environment.  Since V5.4G had been in
     use already for many years, this was a highly important aspect of the decision to use the Year 2000 patches for
     V5.4G, aside from the fact that V5.7 had not yet, and still has not been released as of when this post was sent.

     I feel now that there has been sufficient time in actual production, along with over a year of field testing, to
     ensure that the patches which are now being made available to other commercial users are as satisfactory as
     they can be.  A second and third set of patches for the same customer have also been delivered and will shortly
     be placed into use on 2 other RT-11 based systems, one in the same NGS and the second in another NGS.

4.  Other Considerations and Contact Information
     The use of the Year 2000 patches will likely not resolve all of your Year 2000 compliance problems.  In
     addition to the operating system, any lack of Year 2000 compliance in the application programs must also
     be fixed.  For those using a custom generated monitor file, the fitting of the patches may not be trivial.
     Consequently, I suggest that if you wish to make any inquires, please provide the following information:

          Exact RT-11 and TSX-PLUS versions you are using
          A short, but complete, description of the hardware.
          A few sentences to describe the overall application.
          Your goals as to why you are considering the continued use of RT-11/TSX-PLUS.
          What kind of user you are: Commercial/Hobby

     While this initial inquiry is primarily for commercial users, hobby users are also encouraged to respond to
     provide as complete a picture as possible as to just how much interest there is in Year 2000 patches for
     all versions of RT-11.  It is possible that there is very substantial interest and a need for Year 2000
     patches for many versions of RT-11.  On the other hand, there may be so little interest that it was not
     even worth my while to post this announcement.  If anyone who replies wishes to receive a summary
     of how many contacts were produced by this post, please provide an e-mail address for return.  I see
     no reason to hide from other users what is the level of interest and support for the continued use of RT-11
     by both commercial and hobby users.  While it is entirely possible that there are only a dozen or so of
     still interested hobby users who are really only talking to each other, I would hope that there are many more.
     But, sadly, I would not be very surprised if there were few, if any, contacts from this post.

          Commercial/Hobby Users may send an e-mail to:
                "Jerome Fine" <jhfine@idirect.com>
          By snail mail, you may also contact me:
                Dr. Jerome H. Fine, P. Eng.
                VP Software Development
                Fine Services & Co. Ltd.
                2  Inglis  Gate
                Toronto,   Ontario,   Canada       M2R 3G3
                (416) 667-8233

5.  Hobby Users
     Very recently, Mentec placed V5.3 of RT-11 on an internet site for use by hobby users under the Supnik
     emulator.  In recognition of that aspect, I will refrain from offering Year 2000 patches to make V5.3 Y2K
     compliant, at least for the time being and depending to some extent that DEC/Compaq/Mentec recognize that
     the lack of Year 2000 compliance is a known "bug" which needs to be corrected.  Recently, Mentec has stated
     that V5.7 of RT-11 will be released in November and I do not wish to hinder the possibility that any hobby
     user will pay the upgrade price for an H-kit (QJ013-H3) for V5.7 of RT-11.  The current price for the H-kit
     for V5.6 is, as far as I know, $ US 1600.  For those rare hobby users with multiple RT-11 sites, the
     "Right-To-Copy" License (QJ013-HZ) to allow the use of the V5.7 distribution on other computers will
     likely be the same as for V5.6 which I understand is $ US 315.  If DEC/Compaq/Mentec choose to regard
     the lack of Year 2000 compatibility in the most current version (V5.6) and any previous versions as other
     than a known "bug", I presume that V5.7 is not necessary to correct the problem.

     In the long run, I expect that there will be patches for V5.3 for use by hobby users, very likely within a year.
     But for now, the commercial users will have priority.  They need to get the job done and have everything
     working and tested. However, in order to get as complete a picture as possible of the total interest by RT-11
     hobby users, I request that you make your desires and wants known.  Please notice that I did not use "needs"
     and "requirements", loaded words used by salespeople to try and sell you something you do not "need", but are
