See, here is the real problem.  You say that "the problem *is* the C 
language" in one sentence but then in the second sentence youpoint out
the actual problem which is not one of language, but one of perception.
I once worked with an intern from Marist College in NY.  He believed there
was no problem that couldn't be solved or application that couldn't be
written in APL.  Is it any coincidence that that was the only language 
he learned at Marist and all of his school work had to be done in it??

Most schools pick a language (after the basic course in Pascal here is
done, 95% of assignments here are done in ADA) and stress it as the
ultimate answer.  Of course, the work done in a college course is limited
and trivial enough that the one language approach works.  But the student
tends to leave with the idea ingrained that he has gained some secret
knowledge and now knows the language of the masters.  

It is nothing at as high a level as the language itself that is the
problem, it is much lower than that at the problem solving level that 
the true problem exists.

bill

