> Rant mode off: *That* feels better...
> 
> Rgrds, Chris

I'm glad you feel better ... We agree to 90% :-)

The difference between natural language and a computer programming
language though is one of intent.  Behind a computer programming
language, we have a set of ideas ... and with many languages, that set
of ideas is more rigid, more formal, more structured, more inclusive.
Languages like C give you the opposite ... a lot of that rigidity,
formality, structure and functions provided by inclusion in the run time
systems of other languages are missing ... they must be provided by the
user.

Therefore, depending on the application requirement, the programmers
involved and so on, other languages *might* be a better tool for the
job.  I would suggest that C is not the best language for general
programming ... I would agree that it is the most flexible ... but
flexibility is NOT always better.

I purport that in general, we expect programmers to be systems analysts,
when in fact they are not.  There has been an historical tradition to
combine the functionality of the jobs because programmers can design.  

I was forced to see this difference by, of all people, the US
Immigration Service, who make intense distinctions between the two parts
of our profession. 

Stuart



