Lee K. Gleason wrote:

>   I've been following the discussion on some of these groups, about
> RT-11
> and its Y2K problems, mostly with an academic detachment - I only use
> RT-11 on an old PDT, for amusement, and don't really need the correct
> date for that.
>
>   But them I got to thinking,  I've got a slug of VAXes here, in a
> cluster,
> all disks connected to HSC50s, 70s and 90s. I'm wondering if the HSCs,
> that
> I've heard run a variant of RT-11, are going to deal well with Y2k.
>
>   The DEC Y2K web pages say they are status "B" (OK for Y2K), but,
> don't
> go into a lot of detail....anyone given it any thought?

     It depends on what the HSC does.  The Y2K "problem" in RT-11 is
that the original implementation of date used only enough bits to
represent 32 years past 1972 = 2004, and the system programs and
monitors had no provision for 4-digit dates (namely anything past 1999).

     There is absolutely nothing wrong with running RT-11 and saying
"Today is 1 Jan 80",
which is a fully-legal RT-11 date (assuming you mean 1980).  Even
Version 2 should run
just fine, though the file dates will not be "correct" (i.e. they will
have the system, not the
"actual", date on them).
     If your system is not doing any "date" work (for example, if you
have the clock turned
off), then as long as you don't give it a file with a date past 1999,
RT-11 should work
just fine.  I'm assuming your HSCs run either embedded code or boot from
a floppy, but
are probably NOT date-sensitive.  If so, you'll be fine.

Bob Schor
RT-11/TSX+ User/Abuser




