Peter da Silva wrote:
> 
> 
> It also made TKB a total hog.
>
I remember working for a large UK multinational in the mid eighties
where the main development machine was a 11/34 with 4 x RL01's, RSX11-M,
where if you started a task build at 9.00am in the morning, no one could
get any sense at all out of the machine until well after lunch.
Normally, it supported about a dozen users running edt etc., but tkb
just stopped it dead. 

You have put it all into perspective though - it's a bit unfair to look
through late nineties eyes at sixties / seventies architectures. The pro
series were pretty good for their time and probably on a par or better
than what was generally available from other vendors. How about the 380
though ?. They were quite highly specced machines and wonder how they
compare with other desktop machines of the time. 

The earliest pdp11 users handbook I have is dated 1969, which is well
before my time in the industry. The fact that it is still alive and
usable for real work even now shows just how far ahead of the game it
must have seemed in the late sixties. The design team must have been
chemically propelled to produce something so radical and long lived. 

Oh yes, and for those who think it's dead, an agency rang me only this
week looking for a bod to do macro 11 work (must be on my cv somewhere)
- so it looks like there is life left in the old dog even now...

Rgrds, Chris



