Hi,

	I have a Digital Starion 917, which came with a Pentium100
CPU. One of the biggest factors in our decision to purchase this
system was it's awesome potential for upgradability in many areas. We
have been very happy with this system and have upgraded several
components without a problem. However, after searching for several
weeks for info on the proper CPU chip to upgrade with, I became
extremely aggravated with the lack of info on Digital's website. 
	I finally decided to check with Intel to see if I could get a
definite answer to my question. Needless to say, I was outraged upon
learning of their decision to jump ship on their socket 5 and 7 CPU
upgrade chips. They ceased manufacturing these chips on Sep.30, 1998,
and the supply is already completely exhausted. Trust me, I've
searched for a vendor, to no avail, for the past 3 days.
	They insist that there is no longer a sufficient market for
these chips. They are wrong and they know it. I am angered by this
pitiful attempt to force people into buying their Pentium II, by
choking off our ability to upgrade. The only problem is, you can't
force people to spend money that they don't have. And I, like many
other struggling middle-classers, don't have the money to buy a
Pentium II, no matter how much I'd like to have one.
	However, I have since found out that there are other places to
buy a suitable upgrade chip. I have heard that Kingston Technologies
offer suitable chips, although I haven't looked there myself. I found
my CPU upgrade chip at Evergreen Technologies
<http://www.evertech.com>. If anyone else knows of other alternative
suppliers, there are lots of folks out there who would love to hear
about them. Spread the word, Intel hasn't won this one yet.

Cheers,
GCM 



