>But how does one fight an entire educational system and product structure?

  We have seen two new computer paradigms change the face of the 
computer industry in recent history.  Their pathologies were almost 
identical.

  First, Unix became the operating system of choice for institutions 
which could not afford mainframe systems (ie, most universities).  
Because Unix would run on an itty bitty PDP, and because these PDP's 
were eminently more accessible than the mainframe systems, an entire 
generation of computer engineers came out of school knowing and loving 
Unix.  This changed the way they thought of the operating system's 
organization.  It also made C the language of choice for software 
development.  This and successive generations of engineers remade the 
face of the computing industry.  Only a few systems exist today which 
have not been influenced by "The Unix Way" (mostly legacy systems 
based on pre-1970's software and some newer systems predominantly
influenced by Apple's system methodology (ie, BeOS)).

  Second, for better or for worse Apple changed the way most people 
thought about the computer.  Before Apple, computers required that 
their users submerged themselves into a sea of technical details in
order to make the computer do what they needed it to do.  Of course
to those of us who were involved with computers before Apple, this
seems entirely correct and proper, but it condemned those too timid,
stupid, or undisciplined (or unwilling to commit the time and energy 
it takes to understand computers) to a life without the benefits of
using computers.  Apple went through great efforts to shield the user
from the technical details of the computer's workings, and targetted
the vast pool of "have-nots" very successfully.  Apple achieved the
greatest degree of penetration among non-technical university students
and pre-collegiate students.  As a result, the new generation of young
urban professionals came out of school demanding this style of "user
friendliness" out of *any* computerized system.  It also gave rise to 
the idea that learning the details of using a computer system was a 
valuable thing irrespective of learning how a computer actually works.
Today this attitude is so widespread that it practically goes without
saying, but before the mid-1980's it was unheard of.

  What these have in common:
  * A population of future professionals (ie, people who will be 
    determining how products will be made within their companies
    and/or have buying power) who are denied use of computers,
  * A technology or methodology which makes computers accessible 
    to this population, 
  * A successive generation of professionals to whom that techno-
    logy or methodology is "the right way" of making a computer.

  How to create a scenario matching this template that will do for 
sophisticated software development methodology what these case 
scenarios did for Unix, C, and user-friendly/idiot-usable personal 
computers is not immediately obvious.  The matter is complicated
by the Universities' deliberate sabotage, removing Pascal/Modula
from their introductory programming courses and replacing them 
with C and C++.

  Some possible courses of action I can think of are providing 
very young computer users with the correct development tools 
before they reach the universities (the problem here being that 
the language would need to be sufficiently simple that a middle
or elementary student could understand it, yet sufficiently 
powerful that it would remain the correct language of choice in
