Malcolm Dunnett <dunnett@mala.bc.ca> writes:
>   I'd like to install b. into a. , but the SIMMS have gold plated pins
> and the socket is silver coloured ( nickel or tin I presume ).
>
>   I've heard this is not a good idea. I understand in principle that
> mixing two different metals (along with an ionized solution) is a recipe
> for corrosion, but is this much of a real world concern?

  I've gotten tired of answering this in the PC newsgroups (since everyone
ignores it) but the audience here is better-mannered, so I'll post again.

  SIMM sockets come in two kinds - those that have points to pierce the
surface of the SIMM and those that don't.

  The first kind are used with solder ("tin") SIMMs, and the points poke
into the solder and make an airtight connection, very similar to the way
wirewrap wire on a square wrap in works.

  The second kind are almost always gold-plated, and rely on gold plating
on the SIMM. Since both sides are gold, there isn't any oxidation and there
is no need for punching holes in the SIMM. The socket pins are a little
more springy and don't have sharp points.

  The SIMMS also have a slightly different thickness on their pins - the 
gold ones are very thin gold plate on copper, while the solder ones are a
much thicker solder layer on copper.

  So the problems from mismatching SIMM and socket depend on which is gold
and which is tin.

  A tin SIMM in a gold socket will bend the pin springs back a little,
since the SIMM pins are thicker. Plus you have the dissimilar metals prob-
lem.

  A gold SIMM in a tin socket may make a poor connection since the points
on the socket are expecting a thicker pin, and the copper on the SIMM pins
(underneath that very thin gold plate) is harder than solder, so when you
latch the SIMM into the socket, you can "stub the toes" on the socket points.
Also, since the points don't sink into the SIMM, minor manufacturing tol-
erances on the socket (not all pins are at the exact same height) mean that
some pins may not make reliablec contact with the SIMM).

  In either case, not only can you have problems with the socket/SIMM com-
bination, but the socket can be damaged so that it's unreliable even with
the right SIMM.

