jmarnell@SPAMBLOCKER.netcomuk.co.uk writes:
> 	They insist that there is no longer a sufficient market for
> these chips. They are wrong and they know it. I am angered by this
> pitiful attempt to force people into buying their Pentium II, by
> choking off our ability to upgrade.

  What you consider a "sufficent market" and what Intel considers it are
likely two very different things. I suspect that not only are 10,000
chips not enough, but that 100,000 chips may not be either.

  The vast majority of Intel's paroduction go to OEM customers who sign
an agreement to buy at least <very big number> chips. When a new chip
comes out, those OEM's rapidly switch to the new chip, and most of the
market for the older chip evaporates. Whatever is left gets sold off and
the production line changed over to make a newer chip. In some cases, if
Intel has a long-term contract to supply a particular part, they may set
aside complete parts (or just completed wafers) to meet that contract,
but that's just for that specific customer.

  However, just because Intel doesn't make the chips any more doesn't
mean that all the existing unsold ones vanished off the face of the planet.
If you call around to various dealers you should be able to find some. Of
course, the larger "authorized" dealers will likely have sent their unsold
chips back for a credit, since they don't want to be stuck with a large
quantity of obsolete chips. Smaller dealers who got the chips on the gray
market are less likely to return them.

