I could say that people never used to ... they'd get maybe 6 months to a
year out of a DEC PDP-11 product before it was out of date.  How else
did RSTS get to V10!!!  If you wanted "free" upgrades, you had to buy an
SMS (Self Maintenance Service) or some kind of Telephone Support
contract.  So you still paid for the service.  This essentially updated
your license to run the new version, and provided the media and doc
every time a new version came out.  The longer the gap between the
versions, the more people dropped the service agreements, arguing it was
cheaper to buy a new license and kit than to pay an annual contract fee.

> >I recently bought Eudora email for Windoze and blow me down, 3 weeks
> >after I bought it, without any notice, a new version comes out ... and
> >there is NO discount for someone with the previous version.
> 
> I know the feeling, DEC did this to me when I bought DUNIX except that they
> had *already* released the new version but decided to stick me with the
> previous one anyway, which was brain damaged.  Needless to say that was the
> last time I ever bought software from DEC.

Seems to me that you had a legit case there, and it should have been
upgraded under warranty.
 
> >People buy cars, and a new model version comes out that fixes problems
> >with last years, and the car company makes no rebate for the fact that
> >you bought last years problems.
> 
> Actually I've had very good luck with that, my local Chrysler dealer is happy
> to give free fix kits for the really serious problems (it beats getting sued!),
> which so far are the tendency of the '85 2.2l turbo engine to crack a 55 PSI
> fuel line over the exhaust manifold and burst into flame, and then there was
> that class action suit that Chrysler lost over the minivan hatch latches.
> With the door latch thing they were smart, after they pulled DMV records to
> find everyone who owns one of their vans and sent out letters begging them
> to come get the latches replaced, they started mixing in junk mail too,
> like a $500-off coupon for upgrading to a new van.  Nice way to make the
> best of a bad situation, and it certainly beat telling us all we'd have
> to buy brand new vans if we wanted one that didn't pop the hatch wide open
> while driving down the highway (although, mine never did that in the first
> place, even with an unrestrained TU45 in back).  They've got to give *some*
> assurance that the product will perform, or else no one will buy it.
> 
> John Wilson
> D Bit

Yup ... I've never had a minivan hatch pop either (supposedly it was
only likely if you were rear ended ... thanks for reminding me to go do
that too!  Mine IS waiting for that ECO!)

The problem with this comparison is that you never know what you are
*really* paying for a car, in spite of discount coupons etc.  It reminds
me of the old days of Student Discounts (tm) ... where a store aimed its
products at students, jacked up their prices 25% and gave students 10%
off!

When PDP-11 and VAX software becomes a commodity business we might see a
change :-0

Stuart



