>Be careful where you throw those stones.  We still use Pascal as our
>introductory programming course.  And we are taking flak from guidance
>counselors and addmissions types because our continued use of Pascal
>"makes us look too high schoolish", whatever that means!!  [...]

  I apologize.  You're absolutely right, and I knew it (I've had this
discussion with my ex-instructors at UCSC).  Nevertheless, it's hard
not to be bitter when IMO it's within the college's power to completely
reverse this trend by teaching Dijkstra and highlighting the advantages
strongly-typed languages with large static semantic sets confer to code
correctness provability and code optimization.  In today's computer
industry, software developers are being hit hard by the exponential
relationship between code complexity and code stability, and hardware
developers are being hit equally hard by modern optimization techniques
being unable to effectively utilize all the functional units we can now
cram onto a single silicon die.  Better antialiasing alone could reduce
some applications' need for off-chip memory bandwidth by 75%!

  And I apologize again .. my anger isn't directed at you specifically,
it's just a touchy subject in general.  Programming language semantics
and compiler technology were my joy in school, but much of what we know
in theory is unusable in practice because of C's dominance.

