> > Indeed, they knew quite well the problem was there.  They
> > even documented that there would be a problem come Y2K.  Yet
> > they didn't lift a finger to fix it.
>
> You just proved my point Tim ...
>
> Dunno when the comment was added ... but since no-one thought that
> PDP-11s would be around in 2000, it made no sense to throw the limited
> engineering dollars at RT-11 as of the late 80s.  So someone commented
> that they were aware of the problem ... that doesn't mean that they
> expected to have to do anything about it.
>
> I was quite astounded as to the amount of engineering that went into RT
> V5.6 and into RSTS/E V10 both so obviously near the end of their lives.
>
> As for Jerome's comment ...
>
> There was no desire to break stuff to make the date word more logical.
> One of the MAJOR design philosophies of Digital during the late 80s /
> early 90s was ... Don't break anything because we want to reduce the
> maintainability costs.  If you look at RSX during that period, you'll
> find that many of the changes were related to maintainability ... things
> that would help ensure a stable product with fewer calls to support.

Jerome Fine replies:

It is very unusual, Stuart, that I have to compliment you.  But 3 times
in one day is a record:

(a) You recognized that the original DATE value was not logical,
(b) One the decision had been made, however, illogical, you stick
      with an illogical DATE value for compatibility, and
(c) The lack of Year 2000 compatibility was considered a bug to
      be fixed.

(d) Indeed, of all the dumb things that were done in RT-11, in my opinion,
the DATE value order of the fields was the only one that could not
be corrected.  And although it is trivial, in the extreme, it likely proves
the point that even very clever people sometimes are especially
careless when taking care of something that is not very important -
they become a bit slipshod.  Since I really can't find anything else
wrong with RT-11 that can't be fixed, I do suggest that you try and
find (d) as a compliment about RT-11 and just leave it.

(e) I also agree that changing the order of the fields in the DATE value
would have been a very bad decision - compatibility was far too
important over a matter as trivial as the extra code that is sometimes
required - another compliment as to a decision that was correct.

(f) Aside from your insistence that "no-one thought that PDP-11s
would be around in 2000", a position which either lacks credibility
or displays a lack of even the least bit of analysis or understanding
of the PDP-11 market (just look at how many PDP-8s are still
running), I must again compliment you on your recognition that
the lack of Year 2000 compatibility was a known "bug" to be
corrected when the development team had the time.

I realize that these 3 compliments may sound tongue in cheek
since I seem to be combining them with other aspects about
which we do not agree.  However, if you stop and think for
even a few minutes before firing off a protest, you will realize
that in respect to these 3 items, we do agree very strongly.
It is just that we so strongly disagree over other items that
my compliments over these 3 seem to be lost.

Finally, I also was very surprised to see such a major
revamp of V5.6 of RT-11, which as Bob Schor remarked,
should have been V6.0 or RT-11, except that management
would not have released it.  What I do not understand
is why DEC would not have sold, to a few of the departing
employees, the opportunity to maintain a major resource
of the company and provide the users with future releases.
UNLESS - and here I may be treading on thin ice - the
sales of RT-11 software had fallen to such low volumes
that DEC actually did take the correct decision.  I know
that releasing the actual numbers now would not hurt
DEC, but if I were a betting person, I would say that
DEC still has the same old secretive structure and
will still not allow the numbers to be known.  Which
leads me to bet that the numbers were very low indeed.
Anyone want to disprove my opinion and release the
actual numbers?  Or will this challenge be ignored
because I am correct?

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
Year 2000 Solutions for Legacy RT-11 Applications
(Sources not always required)




