It turns out that the changes made to the RT-11 utilities
in 5.7 for Y2K compliance are small compared to what'll have
to be done to parse CSI command lines with the year 2258
(decimal) in them.  I'm offering a free set of RT-11 DECUS SIG
tapes to the first poster who correctly recognizes the
significance of this "date" and who demonstrates exactly why
this year is *already* recognized by the pre-5.7 RT-11 utilities
PIP, DIR, and QUEMAN, but in an odd way, for example:

.DIR/SINCE:2257
?DIR-F-Invalid Command

.DIR/SINCE:2258
 18-Oct-1998
ALPH00.FNT   9   18-Apr-1998       SYSTEM.MAC   1184  30-Apr-1998
[...]

.DIR/SINCE:2259
?DIR-F-Invalid Command

(And no, Stuart, you can't collect on this one because I
told you the answer to this after your last visit out here!
And since I've already given Megan a set of RT-11 DECUS SIG 
tapes she ought to give someone else a chance for a change...)

Hints: the next "magic date" after 2258 is 2447.  And putting
2447 into the DIR/SINCE: command will always return the same
or fewer, but never more, files in the listing than 2258.
And the magic number 9802 will always return at least as
many files as 2258 did when used in DIR/SINCE:.

Pre-5.7 DIR releases also will take years after 32767 without
complaint, but that was a bug, not a feature!

