> But that's about attitude and self displine, or are we helpless and not
> responsible for our actions ?. The fact that a tool is very powerfull
> and flexible doesn't mean that the tool itself is at fault. It does mean
> though that you have to be responsible in it's use. You can't blame or
> ban the tool because some people misuse it.
> 
> I guess it comes down to whether you think prevention is better than
> cure - prevention in this case being better programmmer training and
> cure being restrictive, retentive languages.
> 
> > Other languages do tend to enforce some kind of structure on you (Yes,
> > even FORTRAN's GO TO!) that makes it more difficult to write bad code.
> >
> 
> Sorry, absolute rubbish - remember the old saying ? - a bad programmer
> will write Fortran (or worse still, Basic) in any language. Once again,
> better education is the key.
> 
> Define the problem and data structures first, and the code almost writes
> itself. Usually works 100% first time as well.
> 
> Rgrds, Chris
-----

You haven't disagreed really with the point I was making ...

It is a matter of using the correct tool for the job.  And I would
suggest that a lot of programmers proficient in other languages can
write garbage C.  No ... that's not a fault of the language per se.

One of the goals of a high level language is to eliminate the dogs
dinners that people made in writing machine code or assembler.

C is NOT a high level language.  It is an intermediate level language
that is being pressed into service as a high level language.  This
creates a dangerous situation when it is used inappropriately.

I've recently worked on a system involving databases, and was written
rather inappropriately in FORTRAN.  It really did require a language
better able to handle data structures.

C is NOT the panacea tool that it is cracked up to be.


Stuart



