Brian Hurt wrote:

> And does this mean anything?  You're assuming that it'd be as easy to
> produce a 600MHz PII as it is to produce a 600MHz Alpha.  Stating that a
> CISC chip does more work per clock cycle than a RISC chip is not news.
> 
> Brian

But this is not necessarily true. A risc cpu has a highly optimised
small instruction set designed (typically) to execute most instructions
in 1 or 2 clock cycles, while a cisc cpu takes a variable number
depending on instruction and addressing mode. For the more complex
instructions / addressing modes, this can stretch out to tens of clocks
cycles for cisc. There is no technical reason why a cisc cpu should get
more work done per cycle than a risc design. The same design techniques
that get work done per cycle are applicable to both cisc and risc. 

As for why Intel have ramped up performance in such a short time, they
have a lot more cash to hire the best compiler writers than Digital ever
had. Probably optimised the compiler design specifically to run the
benchmarks etc etc. Real world problems however, are not benchmarks.

I have yet to see any Wintel box that gets anywhere near a 400Mhz Alpha
in terms of instantaneousnous of response (not a benchmark, but a real
world observable effect) and that's with the Alpha having all the
execution overhead of X windows and CDE, not a trivial amount of code
for the cpu to wade through by any means...

Rgrds, Chris


