mayhew@eisner.decus.org writes:

>In article <702vep$8jv@alca.helsinki.fi>, vhalkka@cc.helsinki.fi writes:

>> Yeah.. the limit was 2-3 64K adress spaces, about 'user, supervisor and
>> system' and a memory management which could switch between 64K chunks. I
>> cannot remember the amount of 11/23 memory address bits.. maybe 22. The
>> previous pdp11 generation had 18, and the first only 16 address bits.
>>  
>> Probably the C/Fortran compilers did their best in hiding these features, but they
>> were there. The 64K limit was a real nuisance with MACRO-11 ;-)

>The 11/23+ (and 350) had 22 address bits.

Yes, but the -11 architecture has an architectural restriction to the
virtual address space for a user.  For most machines, this meant 64kb
at any one time, period.  On some machines which had split Instruction
and data space, it meant 64kb worth of code, and 64kb worth of
instructions...

>The memory segmentation should be hidden by the operating system, and was with 
>every O/S I used.  The 3 modes you mention are "instruction", "data", and ...
>"system"?  The 350/23+ only offered two of those (excluding "data").   I 

I think the modes you are talking about are user, kernel and supervisor.

>believe each mode had its own set of 8 segmentation registers.  (The 380 had 
>all 3 modes.)  The bottom line is that writing code under a then-modern O/S
>with full 22-bit addressing, without having to worry about the segmentation,
>was a no-brainer (and should have been under Macro, as well, unless I am
>misremembering something).

It wasn't segmentation, it was mapping.

>> The 350 was *useless*... it couldn't do any pdp multiuser tasks because it
>> was a personal device, but there was no sw for personal tasks either. 

>Oh?  I had multiple users running on mine ;-) ... there were 4 serial
>ports available on the base machine if you wanted to use them (2 of them were
>alleged to be for the keyboard and mouse, but that was not essential), plus the
>Ethernet option for access via a terminal server.  Real multitasking is at
>least as useful for many folks as multi-user, and the 350 had no trouble
>doing that.

Sorry, on the *base* machine, there were only the console (which was
a bitmapped display and matrix keyboard), the printer port, and the
communications port.  There was an *option* which provided serial
lines, but it sure was not part of the base system.

