patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca (Serguei Patchkovskii) writes:

>Peter da Silva (peter@baileynm.com) wrote:
>: In article <1998Oct14.175332.1@eisner>,  <mayhew@eisner.decus.org> wrote:
>: >The bottom line is that writing code under a then-modern O/S
>: >with full 22-bit addressing, without having to worry about the segmentation,
>: >was a no-brainer (and should have been under Macro, as well, unless I am
>: >misremembering something).

>: s/22/16/

>: I don't know any operating system on the PDP-11 in which an individual process
>: could address more than 64k of code and 64k of data. Please enlighten us.

>Later releases of RSX-11M and M-PLUS supported semi-transparent memory overlays 
>for high-level languages (well, for Fortran anyways ;-). In effect, this allowed
>you to have programs with much more that 64KI+64KD address space (or 64K I/D in
>the 11M case) as long as you were not trying to have more than 64K of code (not 
>usually a problem) or data (more of a problem) accessible at any given time. It
>actually worked quite well. Even though it was not a more-than-16-bit-addressing
>technically, the difference from the point of view of high-level language
>programmer was quite minor.

And RT-11 had both disk-oriented overlays (overlay loaded from disk when
needed) and memory-based (all overlays were loaded into extended memory
regions and called when needed).

Actually, it isn't past tense... RT-11 is still available for pdp-11s.

