     mayhew@eisner.decus.org writes:
> 
> For the moment at least, I'll stand my ground on this one.
> I'll dig out my old Technical Manuals on the hardware to verify it, but I'm
> quite certain that the keyboard was implemented through a serial port
> (buried in the console connector, perhaps, but nonetheless there and usable
> by someone who wrote code that talked directly to the hardware and who didn't 
> care about using the ordinary keyboard/monitor -- which was a good idea,
> actually, since repainting the bitmapped monitor took quite a bit of CPU 
> horsepower, although I recall that writing the driver was fun).
> 

    The keyboard port was a serial port. It was on the Video/Keyboard
connector (J5). Kbd Rcv Data was pin 14, Kbd Xmt Data was pin 15. The
manual states:

	"The keyboard part of the port communicates with the computer's
	 keyboard. However it is a general serial port that can be used
         to communicate with any serial device. The mode of operation
         is programmable"

		Professional 300 series Technical Manual (EK-PC350-TM-001)

     When speaking to the keyboard the port was set to 8bits, no parity,
1 stop bit, 4800 baud.

     There were a couple of mode registers which could be used to set
the serial parameters. Speeds could be set from 50 to 19200 bps.

   (finally found a use for that old Pro 350 manual)

