Serguei Patchkovskii <patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca> wrote:
>Later releases of RSX-11M and M-PLUS supported semi-transparent memory overlays 
>for high-level languages (well, for Fortran anyways ;-).

Well, the Fortran runtime did. In practice this was more advantageous for
code than data, because your biggest practical arrays were well under 64K.

It also made TKB a total hog.

The end result was that, unless you built the program with well established
and isolated working sets that could be expeditiously swapped in and out, your
program ran like a geriatric dachsund on quaaludes. And your biggest data
structures were limited to 64K, just like on the 80286.

I love the PDP-11, really, but 64K was even more of a hard limit than on
the 286.

