Since you mention the 80286 like something better than an 11 I'd just 
like to point out that the PDP-11 arch came in to life during the 70's
the 80286 came in to being in the 80's and really wasn't an amazing 
step above, yet at the same time 80286 was out from Intel we had
68020 from Moto and 32032 from Nats which had nice clean flat 32bit
address spaceses, registers and programming model and not this now
very old behind the times 64K segment model from Intel.  

I guess my point is I hated Intel chips, they didn't get it right till
80486 and didn't have a chip worth speaking about till 80386. 
Comparing the PDP-11 to 80286 is a little unfair not only was PDP-11
very old when 80286 came out, it had a nice feel to the instruction set
much like the follow on VAX-11. The 80286 was a dog to program when
compared to what was available. If it had been 5-10 years earlier
it may have seemed advanced. I have often though that when Bill Gates
started talking about 640K and less being a usefull amount of memory
that he was remembering his PDP-10/TOPS-10 days think about 256Kwords
and forgot that 640K was Kbytes and that lower prices for systems
and memory would take the focus of doing it right and doing it tight.

I always maintain the line garbage at the bottom garbage at the top. 
In other words if the arch/machine lang is crap then it will oose its
way right up to the user and if its nice that will also filter through.
Look at VAX and VMS  clean and clean 68k and MacOS clean and clean 
look at x86 and MS dirty and dirty I sometimes think I have gone to
sleep and woken up in an inverse universe :)

