No that's not interesting at all. The whole point of early Alphas were
the extreme clocks due to simple short circuits. The Alpha designers
won the performance argument as they made the fastest cpus in the
world bar none. That was what they were asked to do. Any Alpha yet
shipped fits this old clock-speed-demon character.

When Intel demonstrated OOO execution at medium clock speeds it made
the Alpha chaps take notice, hence the 21264 is a huge increase in
core complexity but a very modest increase in "clockability" in fact I
haven't heard of a 264 matching the best 164 yet in terms of MHz and
yet it flattens all-comers in raw performance (if you can get hold of
one). This means that with current Alphas you are stating the boringly
mundane at the end of their (extremely lengthy, relatively speaking)
lifecycle, but with the chips due next month you're just dead
wrong. Really. 

> Hmm. I was quite surprised how Intel managed to ramp ump clock speed for
> the x86 architecture like this.

Yes, I agree, that's what I was saying just recently. Pentium seemed
really hot when it leaped from p75 to p200 in a short time, but the
PII seems to me (subjectively, I am a self-confessed amateur chip fan)
to have progressed even more rapidly.

Whilst the Alpha guys pushed clock speed, cache, memory architecture,
I think the Intel guys took courses on "marketing and pricing
commodity processor yield curves" - that is where those guys kick
serious butt.

Chris
