Ernst,

Phoenix licensed the original BIOS to DEC, but DEC (now Compaq) is
responsible for all upgrades.  The Version 2.04 BIOS is Y2K compliant.

You can download the 2.04 BIOS from the following URL:

http://www.windows.digital.com/ntsbuftp/index/epid37.htm

Because the Celebris XL is based on Phoenix version 4.04, you should
have no difficulty using an EIDE hard disk up to 8.4GB in capacity.

You should be able to install Windows 95 from a SCSI CD-ROM as long as
you prepare a Windows installation disk with the drivers needed for
the NCR SCSI chip on-board.  As far as IDE CD-ROMs go, I'm not sure,
but they ought to work too.  However, I have run into problems with
several system BIOSes which cannot recoginize an IDE CD-ROM drive
connected all by itself on an IDE connector.  (Not DEC systems,
though.)

The motherboard itself is not the limit on the upper range of CPUs.
The 54-23606 main board supports two different processor cards:

54-23982-06 supports up to 133MHz with the right jumper settings.
54-24490-01 supports up to 200MHz.

Your system probably has the 54-23982-06.

Both processor cards have clear markings which indicate the correct
jumper settings for various CPUs.

The following URL is where you can download the service manual for the
Celebris XL5xxx systems.

http://ie.digital.com/channel/documentation/pcdoc/celgl5.htm

Finally, do not expect Windows 95 or 98 to support both processors.
Only Windows NT permits this.

... Ben Myers

