Barrys2552 <barrys2552@aol.com> wrote in article
<19981007021436.14631.00003657@ng64.aol.com>...
> 
> Is there an easy way to determine which node is handlng the lock
mastering for
> the cluster. This assumes that lockdirwt is set to zero. 
> 
Couple things:

1) there is no single lock master for a cluster.  Each lock tree is
mastered somewhere, you have many trees around.

2) lockdirwt determines the distribution of the lock directory function,
not the initial
lock mastering.  It does come into play when lock mastering moves.

3) the system where the lock for the root of the lock (resource) tree is
initially
created masters the lock

4) during cluster state transistions, lockdirwt effects were lock mastering
ends up.

5) during normal operations, lock mastering can move based on activity.

I can't really give all the details here.  If you can get hold of a copy of
_VAXcluster principles_ by Roy G. Davis, it has pages of the details. 
Unfortunately, it only goes up to VMS 5.5, having been published in 1993.
But for the inner guts on locking (and shadowing and other topics) it's
about
the best resource.  I wonder if Roy is still at Decpaq.  The reason I have
the
book was because I got to attend a class he gave in San Francisco around
1993.  Great guy (and book).

Jim



