> I don't know what you mean with 'GUI-based scripting', but PSA has
> _nothing_ at all to do with simulating keystrokes and the like.
> 
> Through its OSA interface, an application exposes its internal object
> model. And the scripting abilities of OSA go _far_ beyond what you can
> do with batch-converters in a standard CLI (trivial stuff like somecmnd
> < input.txt > output.txt).
> 

I think you underestimate the true power of UNIX shell - it goes far
beyond trivial stuff involving redirection from/to input and output
files.

I know from experience that 'GUI-based scripting' can often be a
kludge.  However, it doesn't have to be that way.  I know from
experience long, long ago that Amiga had a rather elegant interface
between GUI applications and its REXX scripting language.  I don't often
get the pleasure of using a Macintosh, so I'm personally unfamiliar with
its scripting capabilities, but from browsing
http://applescript.apple.com it appears similar in concept.

Applescript is a real language, with variables, conditionals and loops. 
It can communicate with other applications through a defined interface. 
Each application is responsible for defining operations that it
supports.  Sounds to me exactly like the way GUI-based scripting is
supposed to work.

