   I have had the same questions about US-III since Sun's presentation
last
   October.  Why should the US-III have similar performance to the 21264
   at the same clock rate when the US-III is an in-order design while
the
   21264 is the most aggressively out-of-order design announced to date?
   The US-III should be just as vulnerable to long pipe freezes due to
   cache misses as the 21164 is.  Also as clock frequency scales up from
   tweaks and process shrinks, the 21264 should suffer less from the
greater
   relative memory latency than the US-III.  Maybe Sun is expecting
miracles
   from compiler inserted cache prefetch instructions.

   By the way, the current "disappointment" with the 21264 performance
in the
   8400 should be tempered by the lesson of MIPS experience with the
R10K.  It
   wasn't until new boxes built from the ground up for the R10K appeared
before
   it started providing close to the predicted performance levels (Are
you
   listening Intelprocessor Support, oops, Microprocessor Report ?  :-)

[snip]

All opinions strictly my own.

