In article <6va495$4ni$1@solaris.cc.vt.edu>,
Michael Ridley <dridley@vtaix.cc.vt.edu> wrote:
>If there *IS* data queued up in the telnet path when the plug is
>pulled, then the TCP socket will close as the normal TCP flow control
>actions take effect.  There isn't really a way to get around that, except
>of course to not pull the plug when there is data still being sent.

Actually, you can still set things up so TCP will wait forever for a packet to
get back. That was one of the design goals, so you could maintain connections
while some grunt went out and laid a fiber splice around the headquarters that
just took a fuel-air bomb.

In practice it's almost always better to close and reestablish the connection,
so the end-users know the HQ got bombed. But that was the theory, anyway.

