Monty Brandenberg wrote:
> 
> ...
>
> 
> The pucks produced some sort of quadrature signal which is then sent
> to the card for interpretation.  A microcontroller could be used to
> convert to something ps/2-ish.   *However* the pucks do use a pair
> of simple wheels with led and phototransistors.  I think it would be
> just an evening's work with soldering iron and protoboard to retrofit
> some guts from a ps/2 mouse into the puck.  I've been considering doing
> this for sometime....
> 


  My memory is fading a bit on the various mice we've shipped.  But...

  The quadrature mice (from Hawley) were used on the early VAXstations,
  where the graphics card had a direct connection for the mouse.  It
  operated by sending phase shifted pulses to the card, which converted
  the pulse count and phase shift into movement values.  But I don't
  remember these being in the round mouse.  I believe that we switched
  to the serial mice after the QVSS (monochrome frame buffer).

  The various round mice were made by various manufacturers, and I
  know I have at least one of them in a drawer someplace.  They were
  serial mice and are described in the VCB02 Technical Manual.  They
  ran at 4800 baud, and sent a 3-byte report.  They also send a
  power-up/reset report that identifies them (which is how we could
  tell the difference between a mouse and a tablet).

  The easiest way to connect one of these to a NT system would be to
  cut off the connector, wire it to a DB9 serial connector, write a
  simple serial mouse driver, and connect it as a serial mouse.

  Almost all mice today use a perforated rotating wheel and
  optical detector, I suppose you could hack something together
  in HW to adapt it to a PS/2 serial mouse.  The original
  Hawley quadrature mouse used copper wheels (with insulated stripes)
  and a set of offset copper contacts!



