Remember that this was 1987, and probably earlier that some of these
things were on the drawing board. While the choice of connectors is 
a poor one, it was by no means an overbearing standard back then that
SCSI use a particular connector. Think of the contemporary Sun3's and their
DB50 connectors, or the many DB37 scsi systems, or of course the macintoshes
with the DB25's. Im not saying that it wasnt a dumb choice on the designers'
part for choosing that inverted HD68 connector, but it wasnt such a blatant
violation of any kind of standard as it might haver been in later years. 
Certainly when they were still selling 2100s and 3100 several years later
they shoudl have put somehting more normal like a C50 on there, but they used
to liberally dispense the aberrant cables so it wasnt like it was terribly
hard to find them. 

