>  You must not know any system programmers.  Every system programmer 
>I know makes a distinction between the kernel and application software.  
>The OS *is* the kernel.  The OS and its applications are the installa-
>tion (or, using linux terminology, the "distribution").

POSIX.2 defines the commands in an operating system (i.e. what you are
claiming is not part of the OS).  The "OS" in POSIX stands for
"operating system".  POSIX is done by IEEE.  Somehow, I think IEEE has
much a better grasp of what an OS is than some random hacker on Usenet.

Personally, I have a very big problem with people who think a "kernel"
is an "operating system".  Besides Linux, every other OS (VMS, FreeBSD,
Solaris, etc.) is marketed as a complete system (i.e. a "kernel" and 
extra tools).  By any reasonable definition, Linux is a kernel (not an
OS), and Red Hat, Slackware, and the like are OS'es.

If "applications" are part of the "installation" (which, by definition,
is configurable) can you please tell me where I can find a VMS
installation without DCL installed (and, better yet, what I could DO
with it)?

And I suppose you think QNX/Neutrino is an OS, too, and that things
like the scheduler are merely layered products, because they don't run
in ring 0 and aren't part of the kernel?

Regards,

Terry Murphy



