InfoServer were designed to be easy plug and play operation. They speak
proprietary protocols. LAST, LAD, LAP and MOP.

LAST is Local Area System Transport
LAD is Local Area Disk
LAP is Local Area Tape
and MOP is Maintenance Operations Protocol.

OpenVMS VAX machines can speak these protocols. UNIX systems can also
speak these protocols, ULTRIX machines can speak these protocols.

PATHWORKS, when loaded onto a PC, would enable the PC to speak these protocols.

The concept was that the client machine, OpenVMS, UNIX, PC, etc... was
responsible for interpreting the file structure presented by the
InfoServer.

At one time, I had many PC's that needed to access a InfoServer to get
software for loading. I would access that same file share on the
InfoServer from the OpenVMS VAX machine, placing large amounts of data
into that file share, via PATHWORKS.

SO, you need PATHWORKS, and to my knowledge, we never did do a NT client
with LAD or LAST.

rtt


