TERENCE MURPHY wrote:
> 
> What is the external SCSI connector on the back of a VAXstation 3100
> called?  Is it useful for conventional external SCSI devices (e.g.
> a ZIP disk or CD-ROM reader)?  Where can I get the appropriate
> adaptor/cable?  Is there a DEC part number for this?
> 
> My main interest is putting a CD-ROM reader on the machine to
> load the VMS media kit.  Assuming I can just put in any old
> internal SCSI CD reader (can I?), would this be a better idea
> than messing with the external SCSI stuff?
> 
> Also -- I'm not sure what sort of VAXstation I have -- On the front it
> just says "VAXstation 3100", on the serial number it says "VS42-BW".
> Any ideas what model number this is?
> 
> Thanks in advance for help.
> 
> Regards,
> 
> Terry Murphy

Terry,

Some Vaxstations had a single SCSI controller, so the external connector
also
had the internal devices on it. In these cases you need to be careful
about
SCSI IDs, and total cable length. Other Vaxstation models had two
controllers,
one internal, one for the external connector.

Assuming you can sort out the cables (I have always used anything with
the
right two connectors, successfully), you will probably find that the
controller
is on ID 6 (or possibly 7), the internal disks on 2 and 3, and the CD on
4.
Use any other ID.

The easiest way to check these is to boot up as far as console level
(the prompt is >>>)
and type SHOW DEV.

You can change the IDs on the fly, and repeat SHO DEV to check what you
are doing.
Once VMS is running, you don't want to fiddle with them.

We used to run a DAT tape on this interface, but it occasionally crashed
the
system. What happened was that the tape would get confused, and issue a
bus reset.
VMS waited for the system disk to return, and then found that it had
failed a
cluster timeout, and bugchecked. Using a machine with two controllers
sorted this
out.

Alan Adams



