:> mean it isn't a good idea to support one. A CLI is one of Macs' most
:> serious defficiencies. It is simply much cleaner and logical to use CLIs
:> and scripts for many administrative operations. Even as a user I notice
: 	No I think ignorance is now MacOS's "most serious defficiencies"
: MacOS has had scripting now for some time, just because you don't know 
: about it doesn't mean it doesn't exist.
: The finder and most application are scriptable
: eg.
: tell application "Finder"
:  repeat with w in every container window
:   try -- this will skip over non-list view windows
:   set calculates folder sizes of w to (not calculates folder sizes of w)
:   on error
:   end try
:  
:  end repeat
: end tell

I'm actually aware of that scripting capability. Still it doesn't feel the
same as a CLI-based script language. In fact, I'd claim that GUI-based
scripting is a bit of a kludge as the whole scripting idea is built around
text input/output and altering text files. GUI-scripting is very much
concerned with graphical elements instead of operations. This, I think,
is not natural in a scripting language. I am sure many would agree.

You also cut my point about how CLIs can be much more powerful for many
operations than a GUI. I'm not a UNIX fanatic as some ppl may be. I love
a great GUI and used one for years without a CLI. However, once I familiarized
myself with a good CLI I very frequently use one. Even starting familiar
applications is often quicker from the CLI than searching through a couple
of folders. As I mentioned, I don't think that replaces a good GUI as I
like to be able to make simple configurations without reading man pages
etc. Still we must keep in mind that writing is a powerful way to express
things (which is why it was conceived in the first place..). I would suggest
a powerful combination of both. Currently a combination like that does not
exist. SGI's desktop is maybe closest to it, but the GUI could still use
a LOT of work to achieve the internally logical feel Macs have.

