> Stefan Mansier.

Sure.  It's a PDP-11/23 (i.e., 16 bits, slow as molasses by today's standards)
in a desktop box.  Has a dual (nonstandard) 400KB floppy and a small ST506-type
hard disk as standard equipment (was available with up to 30-ish MB of disk;
depending on the specific incarnation of disk controller it might be able to
use up to 73MB, or possibly even 159MB, which was the largest disk ever sold
using that technology).

Vintage 1982 design, but still lightyears ahead of the IBM PC many years later.
Much better video resolution (monochrome or color) than IBM's CGA, EGA or VGA. 
No architectural 640K memory limit.  *True* pre-emptive multitasking 
capabilities in the hardware (real memory management).  Several operating 
systems available, none of which included DOS or Windows; a couple (at least) 
UNIX variants, one of which I ported to it.

Its fatal flaw was DEC marketing; DEC (a) didn't know what to do with it, and
(b) was afraid it could cannibalize the higher-end machines in their line.
So IBM and the clones did the cannibalizing... Oh, that, plus a "closed" bus;
you had to get DEC's blessing to get the necessary info to build hardware or
software add-ons, or to resell the machine as a dealer.  A classic example
of technological superiority falling (or being pushed!) flat on its face in
the marketplace.

I still have a couple that I wouldn't mind getting rid of, cheap ;-}  They're
in Virginia, and I'm not anxious to ship them internationally.  As for a
picture... I'm sure there are some around, I might be persuaded to take one
and scan it if you don't turn anything up on the Web.

	-Bill Mayhew



