Larry D Bohan, Jr wrote:
> to me, the similarity of NT internals vocabulary
> (nonpagedyn, pagedyn ,PCB, IRP, PTE,etc ..)
> to that of VMS internals,  is downright creepy.

My trusty old PSION S3 PDA has process priorities. event flags, inter process
communication through mailbox messages, IO routines that ressemble a lot the
$QIO services (except for ASTs) and the same time of device driver lookups
when you open a channel to a device.

It has a NCP protocol which provides functionlity very similar to that of
DECnet (an application on the S3 has transparent access to files on a remote
machine, even MACs, and vice versa, you can run a debugger on a PC which
monitors a process running on the PSION etc etc, you can even backup the PSION
over the NCP to a remote node).

Microsoft is not the only one to have inspired itself from operating systems
such as VMS. There are many basic concepts (such as a wheel for cars) which
are used by many.

I feel that NT will get much more of a VMS flavour from the giveaways from
Palmer and now Compaq (clustering, file system, etc) compared to the basic
"wheel" concepts that Cutler brought in.



