Philip Isaac (pisaac@kodak.com) wrote:
: Forgive me for being a little slow but I have never set a timeout on a
: socket. 

: Are you saying I just put in my .login something like  "setsockopt(a
: really long time)", then, I can suspend my system from CDE, unplug it,
: come back a few days later, turn it back on, and all my telnet
: connections will resume?

No. You go over the source code of all applications you might use
(in this case, telnet), as well as over the source code of all 
the applications they may connect to on the remote side (i.e.
telnetd or in.telnetd, or whatever your remote system calls it), 
and remove all instances of setsockopt(,,SO_KEEPALIVE,...). Then 
you recompile, and do as you say.

If you are really lucky, and all applications you are using are
well-behaved, you may get away with replacing setsockopt() call
in your standard shared library (on both sides of the connection)
or with on-the-fly patching using the /proc interface.

Cheers,

/Serge.P



