Unix is a superior, well-tested, stable OS with a more complete and fully-tested
software backing than anything else out there today. And a Mac has always had
a command line interface in applescript and via direct console access, for which
the BSD shell will be nothing but a far more powerful replacement or adjunct.
Adding a MacOS GUI to it would make it just about perfect. It's pretty silly to
complain about the existence of something one will never have to use. And a
stable underpinning to a system means that more energy can be focused on the
user interface rather than fixing bugs in an outmoded, cooperative multitasking
system that's completely reversed the lead Apple had as the owners of the "most
crash-resistant" system. The essence of Mac is the GUI, and the hardware is
fine.  The only chink in its armor is the ancient OS underpinning.

GMS



