> > machines on a friday afternoon and suspended the system. On monday
> > morning I power it up and the connections were all in place. I guess
> > this would not have worked with anything but TCP/IP.
> 

Without going over the code, my guess would be that the window size
are set to 0, which I believe is ok to have indefinite.

You would definitely *not* want KEEPALIVE on the socket. From man
setsockopt:

     SO_KEEPALIVE   Keeps  connections  active  by  enabling  the
                    periodic transmission of messages, if this is
                    supported by the protocol.  This option takes
                    an int value.

                    If the connected socket fails to  respond  to
                    these  messages, the connection is broken and
                    processes writing to that socket are notified
                    with a SIGPIPE signal.

Don't know how this interact with a window size = 0.

It was a few years ago I tested this. Things may have changed, or my
memory might be fading. It could have been xterms instead of telnet.
I need to check this again.

