In comp.sys.sgi.misc Gabriel Morales <Togega@Concentric.net> wrote:
: If we ever revert to a command line interface, I'm staying with an old copy of
: the Mac OS. Macintoshes have NEVER had a command line interface and it'd be a
: shame to start now. I think Apple knows what it's doing, though.

Duh.. that's ridiculous. Like saying UNIX should never have a GUI (yes,
I know.. some ppl do suggest this). Just because you don't need one doesn't
mean it isn't a good idea to support one. A CLI is one of Macs' most
serious defficiencies. It is simply much cleaner and logical to use CLIs
and scripts for many administrative operations. Even as a user I notice
that many things I can do much faster with a CLI than by using even a 
well-built GUI (which are decidedly rare). That doesn't mean I think we
should all be using text terminals. I like a GUI as much as anyone, but a
CLI is definitely a seriously useful tool.

