: a Mach kernel), as far as I understand it.

This is a misconception. The kernel has nothing to do with UNIX whatsoever.
You can have modules, or boxes, built on top of that kernel to support
various environment. AFAIK they will have a BSD-compatibility box in
MacOS X (it better be good and POSIX compatible or I'm not buying one),
aswell as the Macintosh compatibility box for Mac apps.

How well all of this fits in together is another matter. Let's just hope
it's better than NT which also has a fairly OK kernel structure but the
shells built on top of it are pretty poor.

