Jay Ponder wrote:
> 
>  Does anyone know if this style of mouse has survived into the era
>  of PS2-compatible rodents? Does anyone sell anything similar in
>  shape and tracking mechanism for PC's? Is it possible to splice a
>  modern connector onto the old DEC mice and get them it work with
>  newer machines? Odd, I never thought I'd get misty eyed over a
>  silly mouse....
> 

I agree with you on the pucks.  They're the only mice that ever really
fit my hand.  I've never understood why people actually *prefer* the
slim arthritis simulation mice all vendors now provide.  (Place your
hand on these mice as if to use them.  Take your hand away without
moving your fingers.  Have you ever seen a *healthy* person hold their
hand that way?)

The pucks produced some sort of quadrature signal which is then sent
to the card for interpretation.  A microcontroller could be used to
convert to something ps/2-ish.   *However* the pucks do use a pair
of simple wheels with led and phototransistors.  I think it would be
just an evening's work with soldering iron and protoboard to retrofit
some guts from a ps/2 mouse into the puck.  I've been considering doing
this for sometime....

