>>Mark Russinovich, http://www.sysinternals.com used to have
>>on his Web site, a  program that would exercise native NT
>>system calls, calling  them w/ random  bogus parameters,  
>>bad addresses, bad handles, and the like.
>>
>>He found about 200+ BlueScreen holes this way
>>(since then,  fixed  in one of the various service packs)
>>
>>He wrote a later version, that foucused on various
>>virtual address boundary cases, rather than randomly,
>>that found still more.
>>
>>The interesting bit, though,  was that MR tried to
>>repeat the exercise, against kernel routines, but had no luck.
>>
>>Supposedly, the inner kernel  was much more robust than
>>the outer-lying  layers.
>>
>>That the outer layers of NT, the native API, Win32, MFC, 
>>came to be so bad,  i think is  typical MSFT, 
>>good intentions,  flawed execution ...
>
>With the caveat that I don't know any details about the internals,
>its always been my impression that Msoft just doesn't do memeory
>management for the OS _and_ the user very well.  Is it possible
>that the kernal just doesn't do it?  Their I/O ideas leave a lot
>to be desired, too (but I haven't observed an NT).
>
>/BAH
>
>Sigh! - Subtract a hundred and four for e-mail.

literally speaking, the NT 'kernel' doesn't do memory mgt,
but the 'executive layer' on top of the kernel does.

the NT 'kernel' implements the low-level stuff, such as 
thread scheduling, interrup/exception dispatching, and
MP synchronization.  the 'executive'  handles memory
and process/thread mgt, security && I/O using kernel
routines as needed.

yeah, but I have to agree, NT systems do seem to get 
unstable once they've hit a low memory situation;

for ex,  for a long time NT supposedly still has a bad file 
caching bug, still not addressed in SP3 (afaik), 
that chews up (and doesn't return) page file space, 
when copying  large files.

another one, maybe loosely related to the 1st, is a bad 
page file leak,  each time a page file is extended.  ie, 
each time you get one of those msg-boxes:
   "Your  system is running low on virtual memory"
(one can set min/max values on page file size in NT)

NT v3.1 hit the streets siz (6!) years ago; 
and its's still "Not There" yet, despite 2-3 rewrites.

and Gates, Allen, && Ballmer are still on the top-10 
of Forbe's richest-men list  ...

anyway, the following is interesting NT reading.  

	Inside Windows NT, 2nd Ed, 
	David Solomon, ISBN 1-57231-677-2

to me, the similarity of NT internals vocabulary
(nonpagedyn, pagedyn ,PCB, IRP, PTE,etc ..)
to that of VMS internals,  is downright creepy.

